UID:event-assignment-7096137
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260226
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Technological Culture & Knowledge\n\nWhat does navigating a tec
 hnological culture require today? In the 1970s\, writer and critic John Be
 rger looked at the contemporary role of art as a way of comprehending the 
 aesthetics and politics at play in the world. He argued that we comprehend
  this\, not necessarily by looking at paintings\, but by looking at oursel
 ves looking at artworks in a way nobody had seen before. \n\nSimilarly\, 
 today the technological lens is bringing into focus a world that nobody ha
 s seen before. What can we learn by exploring new ways of knowing? Can we 
 examine not just technologies themselves\, but how we interact with them\,
  in order to understand something about ourselves and about the time in wh
 ich we live?\n\nArtist and writer James Bridle has been at the forefront o
 f exploring what these new ways of seeing might look like. Bridle created 
 a series of podcasts modeled on the ethos of Berger's television series\, 
 that explore knowing and being in a technological world.\n\nOne of the ide
 as explored by Bridle is the materiality of digital technologies. The Inte
 rnet may feel invisible---WiFi is in the air\, data is in the Cloud---but 
 there is an elaborate infrastructure that makes it possible. For example\,
  the submarine cable system\, in the image below\, is decidely material an
 d tangible. An elaborate network of fiber optic cables deep underwater\, i
 t is painstakingly installed and maintained to enable our global connected
 ness. The pattern of these cables reveal both historical and contemporary 
 power relations. \n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/31
 6053/files/36973631/preview)\n\nWhy are there many cables between some con
 tinents but no cables between Australia and South America\, for instance?
  \n\nConsider what it means for current global flows of digital informati
 on to overlap with the former outlines of empires. This overlap serves as 
 a hint that questions of digital equity and digital justice. Depending on 
 where you are in the world\, there are differences in how much digital inf
 ormation you can move\, with what speed and at what cost. This has implica
 tions beyond where we can stream Netflix In HD\; it fuels global informati
 on asymmetries and inequities\, of which the downstream effects for knowle
 dge are of interest to us in TOK. \n\nWhile technological developments ar
 e inescapably entangled in specific histories\, they are also changing our
  relationship with knowledge and power today. Our capacity to steer techno
 logies toward human ends does not always keep up with the pace of technolo
 gical development. Questioning the values that underpin technological syst
 ems can be difficult when they are so commonplace that they remain hidden 
 in plain sight. \n\nListen to [Episode 1: Invisible Networks Links to an 
 external site.] (https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/m000458l)(21:30) from Ja
 mes Bridle's podcast New Ways of Seeing for further discussion on this to
 pic.\n\nPresent-Day Empires of Infrastructures:\n\nAfter watching the vide
 o\, respond to the following questions:\n\nConsider the claims made by Bri
 dle in the video about how technological infrastructure is both affecting 
 flows of knowledge today and making historical power relations visible. \
 n\nHis thesis is that technology is extending the symmetries and inequitie
 s of the past into the present day. \n\n1. To what extent do you agree wi
 th Bridle's thesis? Provide a clear stance on his argument. \n\n2. How m
 ight this extension of the past inequities be happening today? Include sp
 ecific examples that illustrate these claims. \n\n3. What are the potenti
 al impacts of this on our understanding of knowledge? Reflect on how know
 ledge creation\, access\, and authority may be affected. \n\n10-9 (100-95
 )\n\nExcellent\n\n8-7 (94-90)\n\nGood\n\n6-5 (89-80)\n\nSatisfactory\n\n4-
 3 (79-70)\n\nNeeds Improvement\n\n2-1 (69-60)\n\nInadequate\n\nArgumentati
 on\n\nArgument is clear\, supported by real-life example(s) and are e
 ffectively evaluated\; counterclaim is extensively explored. \n\nArgume
 nt is clear\, supported by real-life example(s) which are evaluated\;
  a counterclaim is identified and explored. \n\nSome part of the argum
 ent are clear and supported by a example\; a counterclaim is identified.
 \n\nArguments are offered but are unclear and/or not supported by effec
 tive examples. \n\nAssertions are offered but are not supported. \n\n 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Technological Culture & Knowledge [IB Theory of Knowledge - Barron 
 - YL - 25/26]
