DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260226
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Analyzing the Wall Street Crash\n------------------------------
 -\n\nBackground Information:\n\nThe 1920s saw many Americans trying to 'ge
 t rich quick' by buying shares in companies. This was called 'speculation'
  - gambling that the value of companies would keep increasing and so they 
 would be able to sell shares quickly and make a profit. Many Americans wer
 e borrowing money to do this\, confident that they would always be able to
  pay it back.\n\nHowever\, the US economy was not as strong as the stock m
 arket seemed to imply. In October 1929\, the Stock market crashed and wipe
 d out the savings of millions of Americans.\n\nSpeculation on the Stock Ma
 rket:\n\nThe stock market is a place where people buy and sell shares\, or
  little parts of companies. Companies offer these shares for sale so they 
 can get money to improve their businesses.\n\nShares can go up in value\, 
 so when they’re sold again\, people can get more money back than they or
 iginally spent.\n\nIn the 1920s\, buying shares in companies became a craz
 e as it seemed\, in the booming economy of the 1920s\, that shares could o
 nly go up in value (see graph above) and so was an excellent way to make m
 oney. This led to speculation. Speculation is a form of gambling. Specula
 tors did not intend to keep their shares for long\; they intended to borro
 w money to buy shares and then sell them again as soon as the price rose. 
 As you will see from the videos below\, they did not even need to borrow a
 ll of the money\; they could buy 'on the margin'.\n\n[image.png] (https://
 ucps.instructure.com/courses/368381/files/41326675/preview)\n\nThrough mos
 t of the 1920s\, the rise in share prices was quite stable. But in 1928 sp
 eculation really took hold. Demand for shares was at an all time high and 
 prices were rising at an unprecedented rate as you can see from the table 
 below. By 1929 more than a million Americans owned shares and were confide
 nt that prices would keep on rising. The government did nothing to regulat
 e or control this situation.\n\nConfidence was key here. Once people start
 ed to loose confidence and start selling\, then panic spread....\n\nUnderl
 ying Weaknesses in the US Economy:\n\nBy the late 1920s\, some of the prob
 lems that you have already identified were becoming worse. There were othe
 r factors affecting the economy as well:\n\n* the demand for new houses ha
 d slowed down meaning less need for building materials and construction wo
 rking\n\n* consumers were buying fewer consumer goods such as cars\; howev
 er companies still had many goods to sell. Workers had to be laid off\n\n*
  farm incomes had fallen to $8 billion by 1928 (from $22 billion in 1919)\
 n\n* The wealthy were spending huge amounts on the stock market rather tha
 n manufactured goods\n\n* Despite the profits of businesses\, wages of wor
 kers had not not been raised\n\n* high tariffs had meant other countries a
 lso putting up tariffs\; they were thus buying fewer US goods\n\n* the USA
  was giving loans to Germany to help it repay reparations to Britain and F
 rance after the Treaty of Versailles\; Britain needed the money from Germa
 ny to pay back the US their war debts to the US banks!\n\nThe Crash:\n\nDe
 spite the problems in the economy\, the stock market continued to rise thr
 oughout 1928. However\, in September 1929\, some investors started to get 
 nervous and to sell their shares. As word spread about the falling profits
  of companies\, more and more people began to sell their shares. This affe
 cted the price of the shares which started to fall. The confidence in the 
 stock market had gone.\n\nOn 24 October 1929\, 13 million shares were sold
  on the Wall Street Stock Exchange and share prices in nearly all companie
 s began to drop. This was Black Thursday and the start of the 'crash'. On 
 29th October\, there was another panic to sell shares - at any price. The 
 average price of shares dropped 40 cents and shareholders lost a total of 
 $8000 million.\n\nAssignment Directions:\n\n* Please make [a personal copy
  of the assignment document] (https://docs.google.com/document/d/1PnBz7N0B
 t7hwykWUkHPbZhhwZOeemUJ2MTuB_OwFRf0/copy). \n\n* We'll watch the videos t
 ogether in class but if you are absent today or need to go back and revie
 w them\, the videos are linked in the document.\n\n* Submit your work to C
 anvas as a .pdf when you are done. 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:The Wall Street Crash and the Great Depression [IB History of the A
 mericas HL - Tesar - YL - 25/26]
