 _368381&month=02&year=2026#assignment_6480260
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h3 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Analy
 zing the Wall Street Crash</strong></h3>\n<p><span style="font-size: 14pt\
 ;"><strong>Background Information:</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>The 1920s
  saw many Americans trying to 'get rich quick' by buying shares in compani
 es. This was called '<strong>speculation'</strong> - gambling that the val
 ue of companies would keep increasing and so they would be able to sell sh
 ares quickly and make a profit. Many Americans were borrowing money to do 
 this\, confident that they would always be able to pay it back.</span></p>
 \n<p><span>However\, the US economy was not as strong as the stock market 
 seemed to imply. In October 1929\, the Stock market crashed and wiped out 
 the savings of millions of Americans.</span></p>\n<p><span style="text-dec
 oration: underline\;">Speculation on the Stock Market:</span></p>\n<p><spa
 n>The stock market is a place where people buy and sell shares\, or little
  parts of companies. Companies offer these shares for sale so they can get
  money to improve their businesses.</span></p>\n<p><span>Shares can go up 
 in value\, so when they’re sold again\, people can get more money back t
 han they originally spent.</span></p>\n<p><span>In the 1920s\, buying shar
 es in companies became a craze as it seemed\, in the booming economy of th
 e 1920s\, that shares could only go up in value (see graph above) and so w
 as an excellent way to make money. This led to&nbsp\;<em>speculation</em>.
  Speculation is a form of gambling. Speculators did not intend to keep the
 ir shares for long\; they intended to borrow money to buy shares and then 
 sell them again as soon as the price rose. As you will see from the videos
  below\, they did not even need to borrow all of the money\; they could bu
 y 'on the margin'.</span></p>\n<p><span><img style="display: block\; margi
 n-left: auto\; margin-right: auto\;" src="https://ucps.instructure.com/cou
 rses/368381/files/41326675/preview" alt="image.png" width="413" height="23
 7" data-api-endpoint="https://ucps.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/368381/f
 iles/41326675" data-api-returntype="File" loading="lazy"></span></p>\n<p><
 span>Through most of the 1920s\, the rise in share prices was quite stable
 . But in 1928 speculation really took hold. Demand for shares was at an al
 l time high and prices were rising at an unprecedented rate as you can see
  from the table below. By 1929 more than a million Americans owned shares 
 and were confident that prices would keep on rising. The government did no
 thing to regulate or control this situation.</span></p>\n<p><span>Confiden
 ce was key here. Once people started to loose confidence and start selling
 \, then panic spread....</span></p>\n<p><span style="text-decoration: unde
 rline\;">Underlying Weaknesses in the US Economy</span>:</p>\n<p><span>By 
 the late 1920s\, some of the problems that you have already identified wer
 e becoming worse. There were other factors affecting the economy as well:<
 /span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>the demand for new houses had slowed down mean
 ing less need for building materials and construction working</span></li>\
 n<li><span>consumers were buying fewer consumer goods such as cars\; howev
 er companies still had many goods to sell. Workers had to be laid off</spa
 n></li>\n<li><span>farm incomes had fallen to $8 billion by 1928 (from $22
  billion in 1919)</span></li>\n<li><span>The wealthy were spending huge am
 ounts on the stock market rather than manufactured goods</span></li>\n<li>
 <span>Despite the profits of businesses\, wages of workers had not not bee
 n raised</span></li>\n<li><span>high tariffs had meant other countries als
 o putting up tariffs\; they were thus buying fewer US goods</span></li>\n<
 li><span>the USA was giving loans to Germany to help it repay reparations 
 to Britain and France after the Treaty of Versailles\; Britain needed the 
 money from Germany to pay back the US their war debts to the US banks!</sp
 an></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">The Crash</
 span>:</p>\n<p><span>Despite the problems in the economy\, the stock marke
 t continued to rise throughout 1928. However\, in September 1929\, some in
 vestors started to get nervous and to sell their shares. As word spread ab
 out the falling profits of companies\, more and more people began to sell 
 their shares. This affected the price of the shares which started to fall.
  The confidence in the stock market had gone.</span></p>\n<p><span>On 24 O
 ctober 1929\, 13 million shares were sold on the Wall Street Stock Exchang
 e and share prices in nearly all companies began to drop. This was Black T
 hursday and the start of the 'crash'. On 29th October\, there was another 
 panic to sell shares - at any price. The average price of shares dropped 4
 0 cents and shareholders lost a total of $8000 million.</span></p>\n<p><sp
 an style="font-size: 14pt\;"><strong>Assignment Directions:</strong></span
 ></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Please make <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1
 PnBz7N0Bt7hwykWUkHPbZhhwZOeemUJ2MTuB_OwFRf0/copy">a personal copy of the a
 ssignment document</a>.&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>We'll watch the videos together i
 n class but if you are&nbsp\;<em>absent</em> today or need to go back and 
 review them\, the videos are linked in the document.</li>\n<li>Submit your
  work to Canvas as a .pdf when you are done.&nbsp\;</li>\n</ul>\n<hr class
 ="hidden">\n<div class="panel panel-has-border panel-yellow">\n<div id="he
 ader-3" class="panel-heading"></div>\n</div>
END:VEVENT
