DESCRIPTION:Goals for today:\n----------------\n\n* Note from Mrs. Atwood\n
 \n* IB English Guys and the Line of Inquiry\n\n* Write a possible line of 
 inquiry\n\n* Submit a potential line of inquiry\n\nNote from Mrs. Atwood\n
 ---------------------\n\n*\n\nSome of us need to desperately lock in\, bec
 ause there are NO EXTENTIONS FOR IB EXAMS.\n\n*\n\nIf something is due for
  an IB exam\, there is no moving that deadline.\n\n*\n\nUse your time wise
 ly\, especially in class and in study hall.\n\n*\n\nJust because I'm not t
 here doesn't mean you can use today as a free day.\n\n*\n\nWe don't have t
 ime for free days.\n\nIB English Guys and the Line of Inquiry\n-----------
 ----------------------------\n\n*\n\nToday\, you're going to focus on narr
 owing down your line of inquiry into something sustainable.\n\n*\n\nOn Wed
 nesday\, we followed what the IB English Guys suggested and identified pos
 sible authorial choices and rich ideas within the four available literary 
 and nonliterary works we're read so far\, which are on the IB Year 1 white
 board:\n\n"Black Mirror"\n\nThe Things They Carried\n\nA Doll's House\n\n"
 DAMN."\n\n* Today\, I want you to watch the remainder of the IB English Gu
 ys video about things to keep in mind for the Line of Inquiry\n\n*\n\n*\n\
 nIf you were not here on Wednesday\, start at the beginning of the video.\
 n\n*\n\nIf you were here on Wednesday\, start at 05:50 when they begin to 
 discuss a Line of Inquiry.\n\n*\n\n* [(If the video isn't loading\, click 
 here).] (https://youtu.be/CL05zAPCsGc?si=IeaCO4-W83pqkVk4)\n\n \n\nWrite 
 a possible line of inquiry\n--------------------------------\n\n*\n\nThe t
 wo most important parts of the line of inquiry for a successful HLE are:\n
 \n*\n\nthe authorial choices\n\n*\n\nthe argument (the rich idea)\n\n*\n\n
 Start with the argument you want to make about the work you've chosen.\n\n
 * Once you know what argument you want to discuss\, ask ourself how the a
 uthor chose to portray that idea.\n\n*\n\n*\n\n[(If the video isn't loadin
 g\, click here.)] (https://youtu.be/AowxJIXqR2w?si=FEXTq6_IArNLWOrJ)\n\n*\
 n\nWhen Andrew and Dave say "rich idea\," they mean the argument you're ma
 king in the HLE.\n\nSubmit a potential line of inquiry\n------------------
 ----------------\n\n*\n\nWriting a sustainable line of inquiry is like a f
 ormula\, which takes practice.\n\n*\n\nToday\, you are going to use the HL
 E line of inquiry formula to write a potential line of inquiry\, which wil
 l be submitted here.\n\n* Try your best to write a stellar line of inquiry
  that focuses on an argument / rich idea and authorial choices.\n\n*\n\nY
 our real line of inquiry will be due on Wednesday.\n\n \n\nverb question
  +\n\nauthor +\n\ntext title +\n\ngenre +\n\nliterary OR linguistic aspect
  +\n\nrich idea +\n\nprecise context\n\nHow does\, To what extent does\, T
 o what effect does\, etc.\n\nWho is the author or what is their role in th
 e text?\n\nWhat is the title of the work or collection being discussed?\n\
 nWhat genre does the text belong to?\n\nWhat is the tool the author has us
 ed that you’re discussing?\n\n[(Check page 3 of the HLE packet to see ex
 amples)] (https://docs.google.com/document/d/1ssHqYbplOe9rVhuhO_bvNaPhTSrS
 VPQ8pJC8HzVFXV0/edit?usp=sharing)\n\nWhat’s the idea that you’re tryin
 g to prove?\n\nWhat context is your rich idea happening in?\n\n*\n\nExampl
 es:\n\n*\n\nHow does Margaret Atwood use poetic conventions in her poetry 
 collection Dearly in order to critique political decisions that have adver
 se consequences for the average citizen?\n\n* Poetic conventions: simile\,
  metaphor\, rhyme scheme\, structure\, aposiopesis\, etc.\n\n*\n\nTo what 
 extent does Naughty Dog’s video game “The Last of Us: Part II” use n
 arrative perspective in order to challenge the player’s preconceived not
 ions of protagonist and antagonists?\n\n* Narrative perspective: switching
  the narrator of the story between characters\n\n*\n\nHow does Céline Sci
 amma use the allusion of Orpheus and Eurydice in her film “Portrait of a
  Lady on Fire” in order to criticize the lack of autonomy that women had
  over their own fates in the 18th century?\n\n* Allusion: reference to an 
 outside text\, typically Biblical or mythological in nature\n\n*\n\nHow do
 es Elie Wiesel use syntactic choices in his memoir Night in order to facil
 itate the reader in their interpretations of his implied ideas?\n\n*\n\nSy
 ntactic choices: sentence structure\, punctuation\, paragraph breaks\, etc
 .
