CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Who was Responsible for the Wall Street Crash?\n---------------
 -------------------------------\n\nDiscussion Assignment:\n---------------
 -------\n\n* Share your thoughts on whether it is possible to blame any 
 groups for the Wall Street Crash.\n\n*\n\nConsider the following:\n\n* Wor
 kers (too willing to spend their earnings on the stock market and too will
 ing to 'buy on the margin'?)\n\n* Unions (failure to increase membership\,
  highlight inequalities or to fight for higher wages)\n\n* Big bankers (fa
 ilure to issue warnings that the market was too high)\n\n* Businessmen (to
 p executives received high bonus payments\, there was evidence of bond pri
 ce rigging)\n\n* Europeans (failure to pay their debts to America)\n\n* US
  government (lack of government regulation and then Hoover's focus on volu
 ntary methods to solve the crisis  after the Wall Street Crash)\n\n* Base
 d on what you have learned so far\, come up with 1-2 Questions you have. 
 Be prepared to discuss them!\n\n* Respond to 2 colleagues - either provi
 de answers/thoughts/insight in response to one of their questions\, or add
  your contribution to a discussion already in progress.\n\nResponses shou
 ld be...\n A quality peer response will not make a generic statement such
  as "Good job!" or "I liked it." (You can say "I liked what you had to say
  about... " and further explain). It will provide specific feedback on exa
 ctly what the student did well.  It will add instructional content to a s
 pecific point or present an alternate opinion. (You can say "I wonder if y
 ou considered...."). A quality peer review will make a polite and construc
 tive suggestion for improvement using evidence from a respected source.
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Who was Responsible for the Crash? [IB History of the Americas HL -
  Tesar - YL - 25/26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://ucps.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
