X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h3 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Who w
 as Responsible for the Wall Street Crash?</strong></h3>\n<h4><span><strong
 >Discussion Assignment</strong>:</span></h4>\n<ol start="1" type="1">\n<li
 ><strong>Share your thoughts</strong><span>&nbsp\;</span>on&nbsp\;<span st
 yle="text-decoration: underline\;">whether it is possible to blame any gro
 ups for the Wall Street Crash</span>.\n<ol type="1">\n<li>\n<p><span>Consi
 der the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>Workers (too willing to spe
 nd their earnings on the stock market and too willing to 'buy on the margi
 n'?)</span></li>\n<li><span>Unions (failure to increase membership\, highl
 ight inequalities or to fight for higher wages)</span></li>\n<li><span>Big
  bankers (failure to issue warnings that the market was too high)</span></
 li>\n<li><span>Businessmen (top executives received high bonus payments\, 
 there was evidence of bond price rigging)</span></li>\n<li><span>Europeans
  (failure to pay their debts to America)</span></li>\n<li><span>US governm
 ent (lack of government regulation and then Hoover's focus on voluntary me
 thods to solve the crisis&nbsp\; after the Wall Street Crash)</span></li>\
 n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Based on what you have learned so far\, c
 ome up with<span>&nbsp\;</span><strong>1-2 Questions</strong> you have. Be
  prepared to discuss them!</li>\n<li>Respond to&nbsp\;<strong>2 colleagues
 </strong><span>&nbsp\;</span>- either provide answers/thoughts/insight in 
 response to one of their questions\, or add your contribution to a discuss
 ion already in progress.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Responses</em><span>&nbsp\;</
 span>should be...<br>&nbsp\;A quality peer response will not make a generi
 c statement such as "Good job!" or "I liked it." (You can say "I liked wha
 t you had to say about... " and further explain). It will provide specific
  feedback on exactly what the student did well. &nbsp\;It will add instruc
 tional content to a specific point or present an alternate opinion. (You c
 an say "I wonder if you considered...."). A quality peer review will make 
 a polite and constructive suggestion for improvement using evidence from a
  respected source.</p>
