DTSTAMP:20260309T163000Z
UID:event-calendar-event-1280130
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260309
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:-----------------------------------\nKNOWLEDGE\, LANGUAGE\, and
  TECHNOLOGY\n-----------------------------------\n\nIn digital spaces\, la
 nguage is not shaped only by human speakers. It is also shaped by platform
  rules\, recommendation systems\, moderation systems\, and algorithmic vis
 ibility. One recent term for this phenomenon is algospeak: language that i
 s deliberately modified to avoid automated detection or to adapt to platfo
 rm conditions.\n\nCore TOK Question: To what extent do technological syste
 ms influence the knowledge that can be communicated through language?\n\n
  \n\nTOK JOURNAL ENTRY :\n-------------------\n\n"The limits of my langua
 ge mean the limits of my world."\n----------------------------------------
 ----------------\n\nLudwig Wittgenstein\n-------------------\n\nTo what ex
 tent can technology influence the way language evolves?\n\nIf algorithms s
 hape language\, do they also shape what knowledge can be communicated?\n\n
 WHERE DO YOU STAND ON THE SPECTRUM?\n-----------------------------------\n
 \nLANGUAGE IS SHAPED MAINLY BY HUMAN SPEAKERS  | LANGUAGE IS INCREASINGLY
  SHAPED BY TECHNOLOGICAL SYSTEMS\n\nImagine the classroom represents the e
 xtent to which technology influences language in modern life.\n\nOne side 
 represents language as primarily human\, social\, and cultural\; the other
  side represents language as increasingly structured by algorithms\, moder
 ation systems\, and digital incentives.\n\nWhere would you position yourse
 lf right now? Stand there.\n\nALGOSPEAK\n---------\n\nToday\, we will exam
 ine three perspectives on algospeak and how digital platforms shape langua
 ge and knowledge.\n\nPERSPECTIVE A\n\nPERSPECTIVE B\n\nPERSPECTIVE C\n\nAl
 gorithms are reshaping language in real time.\nSocial media is creating ne
 w patterns of vocabulary\, style\, and expression.\n\nThis is not entirely
  new.\nLanguage has always adapted to censorship\, constraints\, and socia
 l pressures.\n\nThe issue is also power.\nAlgospeak is not just a creative
  adaptation\; it can spread ideology\, produce context collapse\, and appr
 opriate language from specific communities.\n\nSource: TED Talk (Adam Alek
 sic)\n\nSource: Helena Aeberli article\n\nSource: Helena Aeberli article\n
 \n \n\nLINGUISTIC TOOLBOX\n------------------\n\nAs you work today\, use 
 the following concepts precisely:\n\nEuphemism\n\nA milder or indirect ter
 m used in place of a more direct one.\n\nOrthographic variation\n\nIntenti
 onal changes to spelling\, symbols\, spacing\, or punctuation.\n\nSemantic
  shift\n\nA change in the meaning or associations of a word over time.\n\n
 Register\n\nA variety of language used for a particular audience\, purpose
 \, or context.\n\nCoded language\n\nLanguage whose meaning is partly conce
 aled from outsiders or systems.\n\nContext collapse\n\nWhen language escap
 es its original community or audience and is interpreted differently elsew
 here.\n\nLinguistic appropriation\n\nWhen language from a particular group
  is taken up by outsiders and stripped from its cultural context.\n\nCLASS
  ACTIVITY 1: TED TALK — WHAT IS ALGOSPEAK?\n----------------------------
 -------------------\n\nWatch Adam Aleksic’s TED Talk. As you watch\, ide
 ntify the core claims he makes about how algorithms shape language.\n\n***
 *****************************\nLanguage & Technology: Algospeak\n*********
 ***********************\n\nViewing Questions:\n\n* What is algospeak\, acc
 ording to Aleksic?\n\n* What examples does he give of technology influenci
 ng vocabulary\, style\, or expression?\n\n* To what extent does he frame a
 lgospeak as creative adaptation rather than decline?\n\n* What assumptions
  does this perspective make about language change?\n\nMini-Visual Organize
 r:\n\nAlgorithms → platform incentives/moderation → linguistic adaptat
 ion → changes in communication → possible changes in knowledge circula
 tion\n\nCLASS ACTIVITY 2: ANNOTATING A COUNTER-PERSPECTIVE ARTICLE\n------
 ----------------------------------------------------\n\nNow read Helena Ae
 berli’s article and annotate for two different perspectives that challen
 ge or complicate the TED Talk.\n\nArticle: [Viral Language_ Helena Aeberli
  looks of rizz in Adam Aleksic’s Algospeak_ How Social Media is Transfor
 ming the Future of Language.pdf] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/355
 382/files/44924049?verifier=bsouYoNfGCvuCCIMg7Ke5O9dPeMUnepbbAwmCnZM&wrap=
 1)\n\nAnnotation Directions:\n\nBLUE = Perspective B: language has alwa
 ys adapted to constraints. Look for places where the author suggests that 
 aglospeak is simply another version of a long-standing linguistic languag
 e.\n\nORANGE = Perspective C: language is also about power\, ideology\, an
 d context collapse. Look for places that suggest that algospeak is not ju
 st about creativity but also about power\, influence\, and social conseque
 nces.\n\nGREEN = places where Aeberli agrees with or partially validates A
 leksic. Look for places where the article appears to support the idea tha
 t algorithms shape language\, platforms influence communication\, and soc
 ial media environments influence vocabulary. \n\nPURPLE = linguistic conc
 epts or terms from our toolbox. Highlight examples of euphemism\, orthog
 raphic variations (creative spelling)\, semantic shift (changes in meaning
 )\, coded language\, register change\, and slang formation. \n\nSpecific
  Questions for Perspective B:\n\n* Which historical examples does Aeberli 
 use to show that algospeak is not entirely new?\n\n* How does this perspec
 tive challenge the idea that social media has created a completely new lin
 guistic phenomenon?\n\n* What is the difference between saying algospeak i
 s “new” and saying algospeak is a “new form of an old pattern”?\n\
 n* Which linguistic concepts from our toolbox best fit this perspective?\n
 \nSpecific Questions for Perspective C:\n\n* Where does Aeberli move beyon
 d creativity and begin discussing power?\n\n* How does she use ideas like 
 context collapse\, incel slang\, or linguistic appropriation to complicate
  Aleksic’s optimism?\n\n* What does this perspective suggest about the r
 elationship between language and ideology?\n\n* What kinds of knowledge or
  communities may be harmed when language spreads detached from its origina
 l context?\n\nBridge Question:\n----------------\n\nAfter annotating\, whi
 ch perspective seems strongest to you: A\, B\, or C? Explain why with at l
 east two specific references to the TED Talk/article.\n-------------------
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 -----------------------------------------------------\n\nCLASS ACTIVITY 3:
  SMALL-GROUP COMPARISON OF PERSPECTIVES\n---------------------------------
 -----------------------\n\nIn groups of four\, compare the three perspecti
 ves and complete the chart below.\n\nPerspective\n\nMain Claim\n\nBest Evi
 dence\n\nTOK Significance\n\nA\n\nAlgorithms are reshaping language.\n\nTE
 D Talk examples\n\nHow technology influences communication and knowledge c
 irculation\n\nB\n\nLanguage has always adapted to constraints.\n\nHistoric
 al examples from the article\n\nWhether algospeak is a revolution or a con
 tinuation\n\nC\n\nLanguage online also reflects power and ideology.\n\nCon
 text collapse / appropriation / spread of harmful discourse\n\nHow languag
 e shapes communities\, knowledge\, and social power\n\n \n\nGroup Discuss
 ion Questions:\n\n* Is algospeak best understood as linguistic creativity\
 , historical continuity\, or a struggle over power?\n\n* Does algorithmic 
 moderation change only the form of language\, or can it also change the me
 aning and circulation of knowledge?\n\n* Which linguistic concept from tod
 ay best explains algospeak?\n\n* What is gained and what is lost when lang
 uage adapts itself to technological systems?\n\nCLASS ACTIVITY 4: WHO CONT
 ROLS LANGUAGE ONLINE?\n-----------------------------------------------\n\n
 As a whole class\, return to the central question:\n\nTo what extent do te
 chnological systems influence the knowledge that can be communicated throu
 gh language?\n\nAs you discuss\, refer specifically to:\n\n* algorithms an
 d moderation\n\n* euphemism and coded language\n\n* semantic shift and con
 text collapse\n\n* language as creativity vs language as power\n\nEXIT TAS
 K\n---------\n\nTo what extent does algospeak show that technology shapes 
 what we can know through language?\n\nIn your response\, you must:\n\n* Re
 fer to the TED Talk\n\n* Refer to at least one challenge or complication f
 rom the article\n\n* Use at least two linguistic concepts from today’s t
 oolbox\n\n* Explain what this suggests about knowledge\, communication\, o
 r power\n\nLinks to the Exhibition\n\nPrompt 26: Does our knowledge depend
  on our interactions with other knowers?\n\nUse today’s lesson to think 
 about whether our interactions are now mediated by algorithmic systems tha
 t shape the language available to us. If that is true\, then to what exten
 t are algorithms influencing how knowers communicate and what knowledge ci
 rculates between them?\n\nPossible object ideas: a screenshot of platform 
 moderation guidelines\, a social media post using coded language\, a comme
 nt thread where meaning depends on shared context\, or a glossary of onlin
 e terms that changed meaning across communities.\n\n 
