 t="354" data-api-endpoint="https://ucps.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/368
 381/files/41326673" data-api-returntype="File" loading="lazy"></strong></p
 >\n<p><strong>Historical Context:</strong></p>\n<p><span style="text-decor
 ation: underline\;">Aims of the New Deal</span></p>\n<p><span>Where Hoover
  had promoted lower taxes\, resisted national welfare and had placed the r
 esponsibility for aid on state governments and the voluntary sector\, Roos
 evelt’s slogans promoted the three ‘R’s – Relief\, Recovery and Re
 form.&nbsp\; He had suggested that the US needed new legislation to redres
 s the crisis\, a New Deal\, but he had offered very little in terms of spe
 cific policies for his plan. &nbsp\;In fact\, the platform on which his pa
 rty had stood was a call to reduce federal spending by 25%\, balance the f
 ederal budget\, remove government from private enterprise and end the ‘e
 xtravagant’ farm programs of Hoover.</span></p>\n<p><span>In his inaugur
 al address FDR the president stated that the US has ‘nothing to fear but
  fear itself.’ There was fear in the US that the economy might not recov
 er\, and genuine hope that the optimism of the new president would made a 
 difference.&nbsp\; In his address FDR blamed ‘unscrupulous money changes
 ’ for the depression\, but did not suggest that the federal government's
  failures and limitations were responsible.</span></p>\n<p><span style="te
 xt-decoration: underline\;">The First Hundred Days</span></p>\n<p>I<span>n
  his first Hundred Days\, Roosevelt used all his powers under 'The Trading
  with the Enemy Act' (which had been passed during the First World War to 
 give the President extraordinary power without asking for congressional ra
 tification) to pass fifteen bills and set the future direction of the firs
 t part of the New Deal. The first job was to deal with the banking crisis\
 ; Roosevelt then went on to establish the Alphabet Agencies. He said he wa
 nted to deliver 'the 3 R's:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span><strong>Relief&nbs
 p\;</strong>for those suffering the effects of the Depression</span></li>\
 n<li><span><strong>Recovery</strong>&nbsp\;of the country to creating more
  jobs</span></li>\n<li><span><strong>Reform</strong>&nbsp\;by passing laws
  to ensure that the people would protected from such an economic disaster 
 happening again.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Directions</strong>:</p>\n
 <ul>\n<li>Make an <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1wg11vdgHkXH
 nRolWBPmyWHlltE91gE4HeiUze-PG02Q/copy">Individual Copy of today's assignme
