BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20260313T162300Z
UID:event-calendar-event-1289543
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260313
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:---------------------------\nWhat is the TOK exhibition?\n-----
 ----------------------\n\nWith your table\, consider the following questio
 ns:\n\n* When was the last time you visited a museum\, and what were your 
 impressions of it?\n\n* What knowledge can we gain from visiting museums t
 hat we can't gain from reading books---or going online?\n\n* Specifically\
 , how does it help us to understand the world to see material objects for 
 ourselves?\n\nOBJECTS AS SOURCES OF KNOWLEDGE\n\nActivity 1: A History of
  the World in 100 Objects\n-----------------------------------------------
 --\n\n"A History of the World in 100 Objects was a joint project of BBC R
 adio 4 and the British Museum\, consisting of a 100-part radio series writ
 ten and presented by British Museum director Neil MacGregor. In 15-minute 
 presentations broadcast on weekdays on Radio 4\, MacGregor used objects of
  ancient art\, industry\, technology and arms\, all of which are in the Br
 itish Museum's collections\, as an introduction to parts of human history.
 "\n\n* Follow [this link] (https://www.bbc.co.uk/ahistoryoftheworld/about/
 british-museum-objects/) to the BBC series\, A History of the World in 100
  Objects.\n\n* What was the premise of this series?\n\n* Choose one of the
  objects\, and find out why it was chosen to represent an event or period 
 in history. \n\n* What does this show us about the way objects can provid
 e us with knowledge about the world? \n\nNOTE: This series focused on hu
 man history\, an area of knowledge---it is recommended that the TOK exhibi
 tion commentary is written within the context of an optional theme (politi
 cs or technology) and the core theme (knowledge and the knower). \n\nActi
 vity 2: Brainstorm Objects that Represent Knowledge\n---------------------
 ----------------------------------\n\n* With a partner\, think about what 
 you've learned today\, either inside or outside of school. \n\n* What obj
 ect would you choose to represent a new idea\, issue\, or thinker that you
 've come across?\n\n* Which aspect of TOK does your object link to most cl
 osely? \n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/316053/files
 /40355685/preview)\n\nActivity 3: Grasping the TOK Exhibition\n-----------
 ----------------------------\n\n* Write down everything you know about the
  TOK exhibition. \n\n You will focus on one aspect of the exhibition\, i
 ndicated by color\, and find the information in the chart and Padlet below
 . Do not summarize--instead\, design a logo to summarize your section (e.g
 .\, archery target to represent the assessment targets\; a pen to represen
 t the commentary\; a megaphone to represent exhibition day - or more origi
 nal ideas!)\, and then identify TWO or THREE pieces of guidance that you t
 hink is particularly significant!\n\n6 groups:\n\n1. Exhibition basics (gr
 ay)\n\n2. IA prompts (red)\n\n3. Exhibition objects (blue)\n\n4. Exhibitio
 n commentary (green)\n\n5. Exhibition day (purple)\n\n6. Assessment target
 s (yellow)\n\nExhibition Basics:\n\n* An individual task\n\n* Written on y
 our choice of 35 IA prompts\n\n* 3 real-world "objects"\n\n* Set within an
  optional/core theme\n\n* 950-word commentary - BUT best seen as 3 x 320-
 word (ish) sections\n\n* Done at the end of Year 1\n\n* Exhibitions should
  form the basis of a public event\n\nHow much help can your teacher give y
 ou?\n\n* Explain the exhibition task.\n\n* Provide you with time in lesson
 s to ask questions and clarify how to carry out the task.\n\n* Be there to
  oversee what you are doing. \n\n* Provide you with oral or written advic
 e on one exhibition draft. \n\nYou are expected to initiate discussions w
 ith your teacher\, and you can (and should) also seek advice from your oth
 e DP teachers.\n\nAssessment Targets:\n\nThe single driving question for t
 he exhibition is: Does the exhibition successfully show how TOK manifests 
 in the world around us? In addition\, there are four specific assessment t
 argets:\n\n* INTERLINKING IDEAS - It clearly and explicitly explains the l
 inks between the objects of the IA prompts\, and explicit references are m
 ade to the selected IA prompt. \n\n* JUSTIFYING IDEAS - There's a strong 
 justification why the objects have been chosen for the exhibition. \n\n* 
 USING EVIDENCE - All the points in the commentary are well-supported by ev
 idence (personal experience\, academic points\, real-world situations\, id
 eas of key thinkers). \n\n* IDENTIFYING A CLEAR REAL-WORLD CONTEXT - The 
 exhibition clearly identifies three objects and their specific real-world 
 context.\n\nIA Prompts\n\n* You must choose ONE  of the IA prompts for 
 your exhibition commentary. \n\n* All three objects must be linked to the
  same prompt. \n\n* These IA prompts apply for all examination sessions f
 or the life of this guide---they do not change from session to session. \
 n\n* The chosen IA prompt must be used exactly as it is given\, and not al
 tered.\n\n* 35 seems to be a lot of IA prompts!\n\n* But don't be overwhel
 med -- you don't need to memorize or understand them all. \n\n* One way o
 f making sense of them is focusing on the ones which relate to key concept
 s we cover in TOK.\n\n* Another way\, even simpler\, is just to read throu
 gh them and focus on the ones that you find interesting and engaging. \n\
 n \n\nExit Task: What question(s) do you still have about creating an ex
 hibition?\n\n[Made with Padlet] (https://padlet.net/embeds/made_with_padle
 t_2022.png)
