 nbsp\;<em>A History of the World in 100 Objects</em></strong></span></h3>\
 n<p><span style="font-family: georgia\, palatino\;">"<em>A History of the 
 World in 100 Objects&nbsp\;</em>was a joint project of BBC Radio 4 and the
  British Museum\, consisting of a 100-part radio series written and presen
 ted by British Museum director Neil MacGregor. In 15-minute presentations 
 broadcast on weekdays on Radio 4\, MacGregor used objects of ancient art\,
  industry\, technology and arms\, all of which are in the British Museum's
  collections\, as an introduction to parts of human history."</span></p>\n
 <ul>\n<li><span style="font-family: georgia\, palatino\;">Follow <a class=
 "inline_disabled" href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/ahistoryoftheworld/about/bri
 tish-museum-objects/" target="_blank"><span>this link</span></a> to the BB
 C series\, <em>A History of the World in 100 Objects.</em></span></li>\n<l
 i><span style="font-family: georgia\, palatino\;">What was the premise of 
 this series? </span></li>\n<li><span style="font-family: georgia\, palatin
 o\;">Choose one of the objects\, and find out why it was chosen to represe
 nt an event or period in history.&nbsp\;</span></li>\n<li><span style="fon
 t-family: georgia\, palatino\;">What does this show us about the way objec
 ts can provide us with knowledge about the world?&nbsp\;</span></li>\n</ul
 >\n<p><span style="font-family: georgia\, palatino\;"><strong>NOTE:&nbsp\;
 </strong>This series focused on human history\, an area of knowledge---it 
 is recommended that the TOK exhibition commentary is written within the co
 ntext of an optional theme (politics or technology) and the core theme (kn
 owledge and the knower).&nbsp\;</span></p>\n<h3><span style="font-family: 
