DESCRIPTION:The outline is an integral part of planning an essay--this is w
 here you gather all of the information and evidence that you want to discu
 ss and plan which order your argument will be in.\n\nWhen writing the outl
 ine\, be sure to check the rubric.\n--------------------------------------
 ----------------\n\nThings to keep in mind:\n\n*\n\nConsider a logical pro
 gression of ideas\n\n*\n\nIt does not need to be in chronological order\, 
 but it must be in an order that strengthens your argument\n\n*\n\nThis is 
 NOT a mini version of the essay\n\n*\n\nThis is for you to organize your i
 deas and gather your evidence\n\n*\n\nThe HLE does NOT need to follow the 
 formulaic five-paragraph essay\n\n*\n\nYou still need an intro\, a conclus
 ion\, and body paragraphs\, but the number of and organization of the body
  paragraphs is yours to decide\n\n*\n\nInclude the works cited on the same
  document as the outline but make sure it's on its own page\n\n* This will
  NOT be sent to IB--it's for our classroom use only.\n\nHow to structure y
 our Higher Level Essay (HLE):\n\nMrs. Atwood's planning and outline docume
 nt:\n\n 
