UID:event-calendar-event-1290000
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260317
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Practice Exhibition & Prompt Selection\n\nObjective: You will 
 create a practice TOK exhibition by selecting an IA Prompt and linking it 
 to one object. Your object will be selected from a pre-approved list. You
 'll write a 320-word commentary. \n\nNOTE: YOU MAY WANT TO START WITH YOU
 R OBJECT\, RATHER THAN YOUR PROMPT\; IN WHICH CASE\, SWAP STEP 1 and 2 ARO
 UND.\n\nSTEP 1: Choosing an IA Prompt\n\n* Read through the list of 35 pr
 ompts.\n\n* As you do this\, think about how they relate to Big Question 1
 : What is knowledge\, and why does it require scrutiny?  and Big Questio
 n 2:How does our knowledge influence the way we construct our values? . BQ
 1 is primarily about the nature of knowledge and how we acquire it\; BQ2 i
 s primarily about the relationship between knowledge and values.\n\n* Thin
 k also about how the Knowledge & the Knower\, Knowledge & Technology\, an
 d Knowledge & Language themes could act as a context for what you discuss
 . BQ1 looks at the core theme: Knowledge & the Knower and BQ2 looks at oth
 er themes (e.g.\, technology or language)\n\n* These considerations will h
 elp you decide which prompt will work best for you. \n\n* What counts as 
 knowledge?\n\n* Are some types of knowledge more useful than others?\n\n* 
 What features of knowledge have an impact on its reliability?\n\n* On what
  grounds might we doubt a claim?\n\n* What counts as good evidence?\n\n* H
 ow does the way that we organize or classify knowledge affect what we know
 ?\n\n* What are the implications of having\, or not having\, knowledge?\n\
 n* To what extent is certainty attainable?\n\n* Are some types of knowledg
 e less open to interpretation than others?\n\n* What challenges are raised
  by the dissemination and/or communication of knowledge?\n\n* Can new know
 ledge change established values or beliefs?\n\n* Is bias inevitable in the
  production of knowledge?\n\n* How can we know that current knowledge is a
 n improvement upon past knowledge?\n\n* Does some knowledge belong only to
  particular communities of knowers?\n\n* What constraints are there on the
  pursuit of knowledge?\n\n* Should some knowledge not be sought on ethical
  grounds?\n\n* Why do we seek knowledge?\n\n* Are some things unknowable?\
 n\n* What counts as a good justification for a claim?\n\n* What is the rel
 ationship between personal experience and knowledge?\n\n* What is the rela
 tionship between knowledge and culture?\n\n* What role do experts play in 
 influencing our consumption or acquisition of knowledge?\n\n* How importan
 t are material tools in the production or acquisition of knowledge?\n\n* H
 ow might the context in which knowledge is presented influence whether it 
 is accepted or rejected?\n\n* How can we distinguish between knowledge\, b
 elief\, and opinion?\n\n* Does our knowledge depend on our interactions wi
 th other knowers?\n\n* Does all knowledge impose ethical obligations on th
 ose who know it?\n\n* To what extent is objectivity possible in the produc
 tion or acquisition of knowledge?\n\n* Who owns knowledge?\n\n* What role 
 does imagination play in producing knowledge about the world?\n\n* How can
  we judge when evidence is adequate?\n\n* What makes a good explanation?\n
 \n* How is current knowledge shaped by its historical development?\n\n* In
  what ways do our values affect our acquisition of knowledge?\n\n* In what
  ways do values affect the production of knowledge? \n\nSTEP 2: Selecting
  Your Objects\n\n* Select 1 object from either list 1 or list 2.\n\n* Make
  sure you are interested in your object\, and can discuss its specific rea
 l-world context. \n\nBQ1 - FOUNDATIONS (the nature of knowledge)\n\n* A s
 elfie\n\n* A visual illusion\n\n* An object from your childhood about whic
 h you have an unclear recollection\n\n* An image of a courtroom\n\n* A pho
 to of an event that seems to indicate emotion overshadowing reason \n\n* 
 A dictionary definition of emotion\, reason\, or both\n\n* A diagram showi
 ng the TOK course structure\n\n* A diagram showing the IB Diploma Programm
 e--including TOK and the six DP groups\n\n* A diagram of the Monty Hall pr
 oblem\n\n* An image that indicates an experiment investigating gravity\n\n
 * A set of statistics about the world\n\n* A mathematical formula or equat
 ion--ideally linked to Descartes\n\n* A social media post advancing a cons
 piracy theory\n\n* Belle Gibson's cookery book or app\n\n* A medical mask\
 n\n* A photo of a group of indigenous people managing natural resources\n\
 nBQ2 - VALUES (knowledge and values)\n\n* A stained glass window from a ch
 urch or cathedral (choose a specific example)\n\n* The UN declaration of h
 uman rights\n\n* A picture of a bull-fighting arena (choose a specific exa
 mple)\n\n* A mobile phone (iPhone or Android)\n\n* A website devoted to sa
 ving farm animals (choose a specific example)\n\n* To Kill a Mockingbird 
 by Harper Lee\n\n* Strange Fruit by Billie Holiday\n\n* The Chat GPT 'Chat
 On' app\n\n* An image of the universe\, taken by the James Webb telescope\
 n\n* A photo of migrant families on an international border (choose a spec
 ific example)\n\n* An artificial uterus for ectogestation\n\n* A fence (ch
 oose a specific example)\n\n* Python (or other) code for an algorithm\n\n*
  A programme for a 2022 World Cup football match in Doha\n\n* A tabloid ne
 wspaper (choose a specific example)\n\n* An online news report generated o
 r read by AI\n\nStep 3: Writing Your Commentary\n\nYour commentary should:
 \n\n* Clearly explain the link between the object and the chosen IA prompt
 \, with explicit references made to the prompt. \n\n* Offer a strong just
 ification for why you have chosen your object. \n\n* Provide evidence for
  each of the points you make in your commentary. \n\n* The key qualities 
 of an exhibition are that it should be lucid\, convincing\, and precise. 
 \n\n* Check the rubric below for the practice exhibition:\n\n[EX2 The prac
 tice TOK exhibition _ Rubric (24-25) (1)-1.pdf] (https://ucps.instructure.
 com/courses/355382/files/45049351?verifier=Pqg4s73VMBMLMe3Qpk8vSbClzD61pHN
 A1zKE3DMR&wrap=1)\n\nStep 4: Submitting Your Practice Exhibition File:\n\n
 This single file should contain:\n\n* A title\, that clearly indicates the
  chosen IA prompt\n\n* An image of your object\n\n* Your 320-word commenta
 ry\n\n* Relevant citations and references\n\n* You do NOT need to include 
 an introduction or conclusion. Your commentary also does NOT have to answe
 r the question in the prompt directly. It is a discussion\, rather than a 
 closed response.
LOCATION:
