CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:How did the Depression Affect Canadians?\n---------------------
 -------------------\n\nHistorical Context:\n\nCanada was hit very badly by
  the depression - though the impact was uneven. So while The Prairie provi
 nces suffered badly\, Ontario and Quebec were less seriously affected beca
 use of their more diversified industrial economies. There was also uneven 
 suffering between the classes. Although wages dropped\, prices also fell m
 eaning that those with jobs improved their standard of living. It was farm
 ers\, small businessmen and the unemployed who suffered the most hardships
 . In addition\, inequality between Canadians were exacerbated by discrimin
 ation against immigrants\, Jews and First Nations people.\n\nThe trailer f
 or the documentary "Catch the Westbound Train" may also give you some addi
 tional insight:\n\n["Catch the Westbound Train" (Trailer)] (https://www.yo
 utube.com/watch?v=ty27gOAAjqU&t=89s&ab_channel=PrairieCoastFilms)\n\nDirec
 tions:\n\n* Visualize your table group as a collection of Canadians during
  the Great Depression: you could be neighbors\, friends\, a family\, roomm
 ates\, etc. \n\n* To understand how the Depression affected everyday peop
 le we will create a fictionalized account of how your group experienced th
 e Great Depression. \n\n* As a group\, put together a Scrapbook (Presenta
 tion on Google Slides) about your Experience with the Depression. \n\n* I
 nclude the following information:\n\n* What part(s) of Canada are you in?\
 n\n* What jobs or careers do you have?\n\n* What was your life like before
  the crash?\n\n* What happened to you after the crash?\n\n* What fears do 
 you have?\n\n* What goals do you have?\n\n* What are you doing to survive 
 the Depression?\n\n* What is your life currently like?\n\n* Your presentat
 ion should include both information and pictures.\n\n* Upload to Canvas 
 when complete. \n\nResources:\n\n* Textbook\, pg. 150-154.\n\n* [Canada: 
 A People's History - "Hard Times"] (https://www.cbc.ca/history/EPISHOMEEP1
 3LE.html)\n\n* [The Canadian Encyclopedia: The Great Depression] (https://
 www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/great-depression)\n\n* [The Dust
  Bowl in Canada] (https://drc.usask.ca/projects/climate/)\n\n* [Canadian H
 istory - Depression ] (http://www.canadahistory.com/sections/periods/Earl
 y_Canada/Depression/Depression.html)\n\n* [Prairie Life During the Depress
 ion] (https://www.readersdigest.ca/travel/canada/prairie-life-great-depres
 sion/)\n\n* [Warwick Township during the Depression] (https://www.lambtonm
 useums.ca/en/lambton-county-archives/good-times-in-bad-times-the-great-dep
 ression.aspx)
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Effects of the Depression in Canada [IB History of the Americas HL 
 - Tesar - YL - 25/26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://ucps.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _368381&month=03&year=2026#assignment_6479869
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h3 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>How d
 id the Depression Affect Canadians?</strong></h3>\n<p><strong>Historical C
 ontext:</strong></p>\n<p><span>Canada was hit very badly by the depression
  - though the impact was uneven. So while The Prairie provinces suffered b
 adly\, Ontario and Quebec were less seriously affected because of their mo
 re diversified industrial economies. There was also uneven suffering betwe
 en the classes. Although wages dropped\, prices also fell meaning that tho
 se with jobs improved their standard of living. It was farmers\, small bus
 inessmen and the unemployed who suffered the most hardships. In addition\,
  inequality between Canadians were exacerbated by discrimination against i
 mmigrants\, Jews and First Nations people.</span></p>\n<p><span>The traile
 r for the documentary "Catch the Westbound Train" may also give you some a
 dditional insight:</span></p>\n<p><span><a class="inline_disabled" title="
 Link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ty27gOAAjqU&amp\;t=89s&amp\;ab
 _channel=PrairieCoastFilms" target="_blank">"Catch the Westbound Train" (T
 railer)</a></span></p>\n<p><strong>Directions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vis
 ualize your table group as a collection of Canadians during the Great Depr
 ession: you could be neighbors\, friends\, a family\, roommates\, etc.&nbs
 p\;</li>\n<li>To understand how the Depression affected everyday people we
  will create a fictionalized account of how your group experienced the Gre
 at Depression.&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>As a group\, put together a Scrapbook (Pre
 sentation on Google Slides) about your Experience with the Depression.&nbs
 p\;</li>\n<li>Include the following information:\n<ul>\n<li>What part(s) o
 f Canada are you in?</li>\n<li>What jobs or careers do you have?</li>\n<li
 >What was your life like before the crash?</li>\n<li>What happened to you 
 after the crash?</li>\n<li>What fears do you have?</li>\n<li>What goals do
  you have?</li>\n<li>What are you doing to survive the Depression?</li>\n<
 li>What is your life currently like?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Your presenta
 tion should include both&nbsp\;<strong>information</strong> and&nbsp\;<str
 ong>pictures</strong>.</li>\n<li>Upload to Canvas when complete.&nbsp\;</l
 i>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Resources:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Textbook\, pg. 15
 0-154.</li>\n<li><a href="https://www.cbc.ca/history/EPISHOMEEP13LE.html">
 Canada: A People's History - "Hard Times"</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://w
 ww.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/great-depression">The Canadian En
 cyclopedia: The Great Depression</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://drc.usask.
 ca/projects/climate/">The Dust Bowl in Canada</a></li>\n<li><a href="http:
 //www.canadahistory.com/sections/periods/Early_Canada/Depression/Depressio
 n.html">Canadian History - Depression&nbsp\;</a></li>\n<li><a href="https:
 //www.readersdigest.ca/travel/canada/prairie-life-great-depression/">Prair
 ie Life During the Depression</a></li>\n<li><a href="https://www.lambtonmu
 seums.ca/en/lambton-county-archives/good-times-in-bad-times-the-great-depr
 ession.aspx">Warwick Township during the Depression</a></li>\n</ul>
END:VEVENT
