CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:How Effective was the Response of Federal Government to the Dep
 ression?\n----------------------------------------------------------------
 -------\n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/368381/files/
 41326658/preview)\n\nPhoto of a 'Bennett buggy'\n\nA Bennett buggy was the
  name given to a car which had its engine removed and was pulled by horses
 .\n\nHistorical Context:\n\nPM King\nAt the onset of the Depression\, Prim
 e Minister Mackenzie King believed that the situation was temporary and th
 at the economy would recover without government intervention. When municip
 al and federal governments asked for financial support\, he refused and fa
 mously said that he would not give 'a five-cent piece' to a Conservative p
 rovincial government. This statement contributed to his defeat in the 1930
  election campaign. However\, the new Prime Minister\, Richard Bennett\,
  did not have any radical approaches to solving the economic crisis and w
 as just as reluctant for the government to provide any relief.\n\nPM Benne
 tt\n\nBennett carried out some reforms to assist with the Depression:\n\n*
  The Unemployment Relief Act which gave the provinces $20 million to spend
  on creating more jobs\n\n* The Prairie Farm Rehabilitation Act of 1935 wh
 ich was to help farmers build irrigation systems and reservoirs\n\n* Highe
 r tariffs to protect Canadian goods\n\n* Work camps for single men to prov
 ide work on projects such as building roads and digging drainage ditches\;
  1700 men spent time in these camps\n\nHowever\, the first two of these fa
 iled to make any impact on the economy or farmers\, and raising tariffs on
 ly encouraged other nations to put up tariffs against Canadian goods. The 
 conditions in the work camps meanwhile were terrible and the payment of 20
  cents a day led to a protest known as the On-to-Ottawa Trek.\n\nIn 1935\,
  Bennett's failure to improve the economic situation led to voters replaci
 ng him with Mackenzie King once more. The National Employment Commission\,
  set up in 1936 to examine the unemployment situation in Canada\, recommen
 ded that the federal government should spend millions of dollars on job cr
 eation and training programmes. However\,  King continued with the belief
  that spending federal money on social problems during a depression did no
 t make economic sense\; he thus only spent a fraction of what was recommen
 ded.\n\nIn addition\, no action was taken to clarify the financial roles o
 f  provincial and federal government\, despite the recommendations of ano
 ther commission. The Royal Commission on Dominion-Provincial Relations was
  established by King in 1937 to look at the tensions created by the Depres
 sion regarding which governments should be paying for social and unemploym
 ent assistance. The Rowell-Sirois Report recommended that the federal gove
 rnment should give the poorer provinces grants or 'equalisation payments' 
 to ensure that every province could offer the same level of services to it
 s citizens\, and also aid that the federal government should pay social be
 nefits and unemployment insurance.\n\nUltimately the economy only recovere
 d with the outbreak of the Second World War\; the massive state expenditur
 es necessitated by the war finally reduced unemployment to minimal levels 
 by 1942.\n\nAdditional Resources:\n\nFor information on the "Dole"[ visit 
 this website] (https://www.heritage.nf.ca/articles/politics/depression-imp
 acts.php) and scroll down to the correct section. \n\nAssignment Directio
 ns:\n\n* Please make [an individual copy of the assignment] (https://docs.
 google.com/document/d/1eW16PdrW0_l9hydHQWCFXsQDIPFU_OJMk-JsQKR0WmM/copy).\
 n\n* The resources you will need are:\n\n* Videos linked in the assignment
 .\n\n* The Historical Context (above)\n\n* Your textbook\n\n* Please submi
 t your work to Canvas when complete! \n\n 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Canada: Government Response to the Depression [IB History of the Am
 ericas HL - Tesar - YL - 25/26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://ucps.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _368381&month=03&year=2026#assignment_6479762
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h3 id="header-5" style="text-align: center\;"
 ><strong>How Effective was the Response of Federal Government to the Depre
 ssion?</strong></h3>\n<p><span><img style="display: block\; margin-left: a
 uto\; margin-right: auto\;" src="https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/3683
 81/files/41326658/preview" alt="image.png" data-api-endpoint="https://ucps
 .instructure.com/api/v1/courses/368381/files/41326658" data-api-returntype
 ="File" loading="lazy"></span></p>\n<p style="text-align: center\;"><span>
 Photo of a 'Bennett buggy'</span></p>\n<p style="text-align: center\;"><sp
 an><em>A Bennett buggy was the name given to a car which had its engine re
 moved and was pulled by horses.</em></span></p>\n<p><strong>Historical</st
 rong> <strong>Context</strong>:</p>\n<p><span><span style="text-decoration
 : underline\;">PM King</span><br>At the onset of the Depression\, Prime Mi
 nister Mackenzie King believed that the situation was temporary and that t
 he economy would recover without government intervention. When municipal a
 nd federal governments asked for financial support\, he refused and famous
 ly said that he would not give 'a five-cent piece' to a Conservative provi
 ncial government. This statement contributed to his defeat in the 1930 ele
 ction campaign. However\, the new Prime Minister\,&nbsp\;</span><span>Rich
 ard&nbsp\;</span><span>Bennett\,&nbsp\;</span><span>did not have any radic
 al approaches to solving the economic crisis and was just as reluctant for
  the government to provide any relief.</span></p>\n<p><span><span style="t
 ext-decoration: underline\;">PM Bennett</span><br></span></p>\n<p><span>Be
 nnett carried out some reforms to assist with the Depression:</span></p>\n
 <ul>\n<li><span>The Unemployment Relief Act which gave the provinces $20 m
 illion to spend on creating more jobs</span></li>\n<li><span>The Prairie F
 arm Rehabilitation Act of 1935 which was to help farmers build irrigation 
 systems and reservoirs</span></li>\n<li><span>Higher tariffs to protect Ca
 nadian goods</span></li>\n<li><span>Work camps for single men to provide w
 ork on projects such as building roads and digging drainage ditches\; 1700
  men spent time in these camps</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span>However\, the 
 first two of these failed to make any impact on the economy or farmers\, a
 nd raising tariffs only encouraged other nations to put up tariffs against
  Canadian goods. The conditions in the work camps meanwhile were terrible 
 and the payment of 20 cents a day led to a protest known as the On-to-Otta
 wa Trek.</span></p>\n<p><span>In 1935\, Bennett's failure to improve the e
 conomic situation led to voters replacing him with Mackenzie King once mor
 e. The National Employment Commission\, set up in 1936 to examine the unem
 ployment situation in Canada\, recommended that the federal government sho
 uld spend millions of dollars on job creation and training programmes. How
 ever\,&nbsp\; King continued with the belief that spending federal money o
 n social problems during a depression did not make economic sense\; he thu
 s only spent a fraction of what was recommended.</span></p>\n<p><span>In a
 ddition\, no action was taken to clarify the financial roles of&nbsp\; pro
 vincial and federal government\, despite the recommendations of another co
 mmission. The Royal Commission on Dominion-Provincial Relations was establ
 ished by King in 1937 to look at the tensions created by the Depression re
 garding which governments should be paying for social and unemployment ass
 istance. The Rowell-Sirois Report recommended that the federal government 
 should give the poorer provinces grants or 'equalisation payments' to ensu
 re that every province could offer the same level of services to its citiz
 ens\, and also aid that the federal government should pay social benefits 
 and unemployment insurance.</span></p>\n<p><span>Ultimately the economy on
 ly recovered with the outbreak of the Second World War\; the massive state
  expenditures necessitated by the war finally reduced unemployment to mini
 mal levels by 1942.</span></p>\n<p><span style="text-decoration: underline
 \;">Additional Resources</span>:</p>\n<p>For information on the "Dole"<a h
 ref="https://www.heritage.nf.ca/articles/politics/depression-impacts.php">
  visit this website</a> and scroll down to the correct section.&nbsp\;</p>
 \n<p><strong>Assignment Directions</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Please make <a
  href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1eW16PdrW0_l9hydHQWCFXsQDIPFU_OJ
 Mk-JsQKR0WmM/copy">an individual copy of the assignment</a>.</li>\n<li>The
  resources you will need are:\n<ul>\n<li>Videos linked in the assignment.<
 /li>\n<li>The Historical Context (above)</li>\n<li>Your textbook</li>\n</u
 l>\n</li>\n<li>Please submit your work to Canvas when complete!&nbsp\;</li
 >\n</ul>\n<p><span>&nbsp\;</span></p>
END:VEVENT
