DTSTAMP:20260326T184100Z
UID:event-assignment-7129796
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260326
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Refer to [IA Exhibition: Creating the Final Exhibition] (https:
 //ucps.instructure.com/courses/355382/pages/ia-exhibition-creating-the-fin
 al-exhibition) for comprehensive information on the Exhibition\n----------
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 -----------------------------------------\n\n-----------------------------
 ---------------\nSelection Object One and Creating Commentary\n-----------
 ---------------------------------\n\nAs you prepare your final TOK Exhibit
 ion\, use this guide to check your work against the expectations for high-
 scoring submissions.\n\nPart 1: Choosing a Strong Object\n----------------
 ----------------\n\nYour object must be:\n\nSpecific (not a broad category
  or idea)\n\nVisible (include an image in your exhibition file)\n\nReal-wo
 rld (physical or digital)\n\nContextualized (used in a real situation—no
 t just symbolic)\n\n⚠ Common Mistakes to Avoid:\n\nChoosing concepts ins
 tead of objects (e.g.\, “climate change” ❌ → satellite image of fi
 re damage ✅)\n\nUsing vague items (e.g.\, “a phone” ❌ → screensh
 ot of a FaceTime tutoring session ✅)\n\nAsk Yourself:\n\nWhat exactly is
  this object?\n\nWhere did it come from\, and how was it used?\n\nWhat kin
 d of knowledge does it represent or influence?\n\nWhy is it meaningful for
  this particular prompt?\n\nPart 2: Writing a Strong Commentary\n---------
 --------------------------\n\nThe total word count for the IB TOK exhibiti
 on is a maximum of 950 words for the entire written commentary\, covering 
 all three objects. This strict limit includes the justifications and analy
 sis but excludes acknowledgments\, references\, and text with the object.
  \n\nStructure: Typically divided among three objects (around 300-315 wo
 rds each)\n\nWhat is NOT counted: Titles\, image captions\, references\, 
 bibliographies\, and text inside the object. \n\nPenalty: Examiners stop
  reading after 950 words\, so exceeding the limit risks penalties.\n\nYour
  commentary must:\n\n✔️ Address the prompt explicitly\n\n✔️ Focus 
 on how the object connects to the prompt\n\n✔️ Show TOK thinking (not 
 just opinion or personal stories)\n\n✔️ Be well supported with clear e
 xamples or references\n\nTo Improve Your Commentary…\n------------------
 ---------\n\n1. Keep the Object at the Center\n---------------------------
 -----\n\n🟩 Tip: Keep returning to the object. Don’t drift into a gene
 ral essay.\nAsk: “What does the object show or hide?” “How does it i
 nfluence how knowledge is shared or accepted?”\n\n2. Move Beyond Persona
 l Opinion\n-------------------------------\n\n🟩 Tip: Personal experienc
 es are okay\, but they must support a TOK idea—not be the focus.\n\n3. U
 se TOK Concepts Naturally\n-----------------------------\n\n🟩 Tip: Don
 ’t name-drop “TOK\,” but think in TOK ways:\n\nWhat counts as useful
  knowledge?\n\nIs this personal or shared knowledge?\n\nWhat role do value
 s\, evidence\, or culture play?\n\n4. Include Specific Evidence\n---------
 -------------------\n\n🟩 Tip: Add evidence for support:\n\nA statistic\
 n\nA quote\n\nA specific feature of your object\n\nA reference to a known 
 event or response\n\nUseful Reflection Starters\n-------------------------
 -\n\nUse these to develop your analysis:\n\n“This object reflects knowle
 dge that is valued because…”\n\n“In a different context\, this objec
 t might be interpreted as…”\n\n“The way this knowledge is organized 
 influences…”\n\n“This object challenges the idea that knowledge is a
 lways…”\n\n Final Self-Check Questions\n---------------------------\n
 \n✅ Is my object specific and real?\n✅ Did I describe the object clear
 ly?\n✅ Do I explain how the object connects to the prompt?\n✅ Do I sho
 w TOK thinking (not just opinion)?\n✅ Did I include any supporting evide
 nce?\n✅ Did I avoid just retelling a story or summarizing the object?\n\
 n[ch6_p438_cle_handout_teacher-1.pdf] (https://ucps.instructure.com/course
 s/355382/files/45169657?verifier=RPBYheiqxU2a4tnOWdYF89JMxmkVjVZm35R4sR27&
 wrap=1)
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Exhibition Object One [IB Theory of Knowledge - Barron - YL - 25/26
 ]
