DESCRIPTION:What were the long-term effects of the Depression on Canada?\n-
 -----------------------------------------------------------\n\nHistorical 
 Context: \n\nAlthough there was little change in economic and political s
 tructures during the 1930s\, the Depression in Canada did ultimately resul
 t in an expansion of state responsibility for the economy and for social w
 elfare.\n\n* In 1934 Bennett's government passed the Bank of Canada Act in
  1934 which created a central bank to regulate monetary policy\n\n* In 193
 5\, the government created the Canadian Wheat Board which worked to market
  and establish a minimum floor price for wheat\n\n* in 1940 the federal go
 vernment assumed responsibility for the jobless by introducing a national 
 unemployment insurance scheme and employment service.\n\n* In later years\
 , the idea of 'equalisation payments' (see above') was also introduced.\n\
 nAssignment Directions:\n\n* Working together with your table group\, cons
 ider the similarities between the United States and Canada in terms of th
 eir responses to the Great Depression\, pay particular attention to:\n\n* 
 Government response to the economic depression\n\n* The economic and polit
 ical impact of these responses (both short-term and long-term)\n\n* Utiliz
 ing your textbook and any online resources of your choosing\, use CANVA (
 or a similar site) to create an Infographic showing your findings.\n\n* U
 pload your work to Canvas when complete. 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:US/Canada Long Term Effects [IB History of the Americas HL - Tesar 
 - YL - 25/26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://ucps.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _368381&month=03&year=2026#assignment_6480296
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h3 id="header-7" style="text-align: center\;"
 ><span>What were the long-term effects of the Depression on Canada?</span>
 </h3>\n<p><strong>Historical Context:</strong>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><span>Althou
 gh there was little change in economic and political structures during the
  1930s\, the Depression in Canada did ultimately result in an expansion of
  state responsibility for the economy and for social welfare.</span></p>\n
 <ul>\n<li><span>In 1934 Bennett's government passed the Bank of Canada Act
  in 1934 which created a central bank to regulate monetary policy</span></
 li>\n<li><span>In 1935\, the government created the Canadian Wheat Board w
 hich worked to market and establish a minimum floor price for wheat</span>
 </li>\n<li><span>in 1940 the federal government assumed responsibility for
  the jobless by introducing a national unemployment insurance scheme and e
 mployment service.</span></li>\n<li>In later years\, the idea of 'equalisa
 tion payments' (see above') was also introduced.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>A
 ssignment</strong> <strong>Directions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Working tog
 ether with your table group\, consider the&nbsp\;<strong>similarities</str
 ong> between the United States and Canada in terms of their responses to t
 he Great Depression\, pay particular attention to:\n<ul>\n<li><span>Govern
 ment response to the economic depression</span></li>\n<li><span>The econom
 ic and political impact of these responses (both short-term and long-term)
 </span></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Utilizing your textbook and any online res
 ources of your choosing\, use&nbsp\;<strong>CANVA</strong> (or a similar s
 ite) to create an&nbsp\;<strong>Infographic</strong> showing your findings
 .</li>\n<li>Upload your work to Canvas when complete.&nbsp\;</li>\n</ul>
END:VEVENT
