X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span>This criterion assesses the extent to
  which the student has developed an appropriate and repeatable method to c
 ollect data that is relevant to the research question. The data could be p
 rimary or secondary\, qualitative or quantitative</span><br><br><span>ESS 
 investigations use a broad variety of techniques in collecting data\, incl
 uding but not limited to experimental work\, fieldwork\, secondary data mi
 ning\, simulations\, online calculators\, mapping\, surveys\, questionnair
 es\, interviews\, observations\, note-taking\, audio and visual recordings
 \, and collecting censuses.</span><br><br><span>The type of data collected
  should be determined by the research question posed and the environmental
  issue addressed. The methods and techniques of data collection and data p
 rocessing need to be explored in relation to the type of data—quantitati
 ve and/or qualitative.</span><br><br><span>The individual investigation ma
 y consist of both appropriate quantitative and qualitative data.</span><br
 ><br><span>​Many ESS studies will involve ethical or safety consideratio
 ns. You must address this\, where necessary\, paying attention to the IB a
 nimal experimentation policy (which includes guidelines on working with hu
 man subjects).</span></p>\n<p><span><img src="https://ucps.instructure.com
 /courses/376418/files/44948298/preview" alt="image.png" loading="lazy" dat
 a-api-endpoint="https://ucps.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/376418/files/4
 4948298" data-api-returntype="File"></span></p>\n<div class="paragraph"><s
 trong>Designing Data Collection for an Individual Investigation</strong>\n
 <ul>\n<li><strong>Data Volume</strong>: Align data collection with the 10-
 hour requirement. ESS investigations vary\, so no standard defines "suffic
 ient data"—it depends on the investigation type and method.</li>\n<li><s
 trong>Methodology and Repeats</strong>: Choose an appropriate methodology\
 , considering the nature of the investigation and time limits. Repeat meas
 urements with a rationale to ensure data supports answering the research q
 uestion.</li>\n<li><strong>Qualitative Data</strong>: Use qualitative data
  as needed to add depth or when quantitative data isn’t feasible. This c
 an show normal variations in observations.</li>\n<li><strong>Example Varia
 bility</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Germination experiments generally yield quick 
 data\; decomposition studies take longer.</li>\n<li>Secondary data from da
 tabases (e.g.\, air quality) is faster to obtain than direct fieldwork (e.
 g.\, surveys or ozone measurements).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Minim
 um Data Check</strong>: Cross-check data needs for processing (e.g.\, corr
 elation needs 30+ samples). Ensure data volume meets requirements for chos
 en statistical methods.</li>\n<li><strong>Pilot Studies</strong>: Test dat
 a collection methods with a small trial to identify and address issues ear
 ly:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Seed Viability</strong>: Test seeds before a full g
 ermination experiment.</li>\n<li><strong>Survey Testing</strong>: Trial qu
 estions with a small group to identify any misunderstandings.</li>\n<li><s
 trong>Sample Location</strong>: Test strategies for finding sample sites o
 r probes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Adaptability</strong>: Be prepar
 ed to adjust your method if issues arise (e.g.\, expanding range\, adding 
 intervals). Include any adjustments in the report as part of your investig
 ation process.</li>\n<li><strong>Data Processing Requirements</strong>:\n<
 ul>\n<li>Standard deviation: at least 5 samples.</li>\n<li>T-test: minimum
  of 10 replicates.</li>\n<li>Standard error of the mean: ideally 30 or mor
 e replicates\, but variations can be shown with fewer replicates using ran
 ges.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid Data Collection</strong>: Comb
 ine primary and secondary data (e.g.\, a germination experiment plus datab
 ase growth data) to streamline data gathering.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div><
 hr class="styled-hr"></div>\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><span style="f
 ont-size: medium\;">Guidelines for Designing Sufficient Data Collection</s
 pan></strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Workload and Time</strong>: Data collecti
 on should align with the 10-hour investigation requirement\, understanding
  that some methods generate data faster than others.</li>\n<li><strong>Met
 hod Choice and Repeats</strong>: Choose a method appropriate for your topi
 c and time available. Include enough repeats to draw reliable conclusions 
 that address your research question.</li>\n<li><strong>Use of Qualitative 
 Data</strong>: Collect qualitative data when it adds depth\, provides cont
 ext\, or highlights normal variation\, especially when quantitative data i
 s limited.</li>\n<li><strong>Example Variability</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li>Germ
 ination studies yield data faster than decomposition studies.</li>\n<li>Se
 condary data (e.g.\, air quality databases) is quicker than fieldwork.</li
 >\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Data Requirements Check</strong>: Ensure the 
 minimum data needed for analysis is met. For example\, correlation studies
  typically require 30+ samples.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous Assessment</s
 trong>: Process data as you collect it to spot issues early. This helps in
  adjusting range\, intervals\, or frequency if necessary.</li>\n<li><stron
 g>Pilot Studies</strong>: Conduct small trials to test and refine your met
 hod:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Seed viability</strong>: Test seeds before a germi
 nation experiment.</li>\n<li><strong>Survey pilot</strong>: Trial survey q
 uestions with a small group.</li>\n<li><strong>Sample testing</strong>: Tr
 y probes or sampling strategies to confirm effectiveness.</li>\n</ul>\n</l
 i>\n<li><strong>Reporting Challenges</strong>: Include any issues and adap
 tations in your report. If data is inconclusive\, show adaptability in adj
 usting your approach.</li>\n<li><strong>Data Processing Needs</strong>:\n<
 ul>\n<li>Standard deviation: at least 5 samples.</li>\n<li>T-test: 10 or m
 ore replicates.</li>\n<li>Standard error of the mean: ideally 30+ replicat
 es\, though fewer can show variation as a range.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><
 strong>Hybrid Data Collection</strong>: Combine primary data (e.g.\, exper
 iments) with secondary data (e.g.\, databases) for a well-rounded dataset.
 </li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div><hr class="styled-hr"></div>\n<div class="parag
 raph"><strong><span style="font-size: medium\;">Guidelines for Defining Va
 riables</span></strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Purpose of Variables</strong>: 
 While not always required\, explicitly stating variables can clarify your 
 investigation\, especially in your research question and methodology.</li>
 \n<li><strong>Types of Variables</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Independent 
 Variable</strong>: The factor you change or observe for different values. 
 This variable is hypothesized to affect the dependent variable.\n<ul>\n<li
 ><em>Example</em>: In a study on the effects of water pH on fish health\, 
 the independent variable is the pH level of the water.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\
 n<li><strong>Dependent Variable</strong>: The outcome you measure or obser
 ve in response to changes in the independent variable.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Exam
 ple</em>: In the water pH study\, the dependent variable is the health of 
 the fish\, which could be measured by growth rate\, activity level\, or su
 rvival rate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Control Variables</strong>: F
 actors other than the independent variable that could influence the depend
 ent variable. These should be kept constant to avoid confounding effects.\
 n<ul>\n<li><em>Example</em>: In the water pH study\, control variables mig
 ht include water temperature\, fish species\, tank size\, and feeding sche
 dule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div><hr class="sty
 led-hr"></div>\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><span style="font-size: med
 ium\;">Guidelines for Choosing Primary vs. Secondary Data</span></strong>\
 n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary Data</strong>: Collected directly by you throug
 h methods like experiments\, surveys\, or observations. This data offers f
 irsthand insights and can be tailored specifically to your research questi
 on.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary Data</strong>: Sourced from existing mater
 ials\, such as scientific studies\, databases\, or government reports. Use
 ful when primary data is challenging to gather. Ensure proper citations fo
 r all secondary data sources.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div><hr class="styled-
 hr"></div>\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><span style="font-size: medium\
 ;">Sampling Methods Overview</span></strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Quadrat Sa
 mpling</strong>: Place quadrats (small square plots) randomly or systemati
 cally within the study area and record species found in each quadrat.</li>
 \n<li><strong>Random Sampling</strong>: Collect data from randomly selecte
 d locations within the study area\, ensuring each location has an equal ch
 ance of selection (e.g.\, using a random number generator).</li>\n<li><str
 ong>Systematic Sampling</strong>: Select sample points at regular interval
 s across the study area for even coverage.</li>\n<li><strong>Transect Samp
 ling</strong>: Walk along a set transect line\, recording species within a
  certain distance on either side of the line.</li>\n<li><strong>Stratified
  Sampling</strong>: Divide the study area into distinct groups or "strata"
  (e.g.\, by land use type) and sample randomly within each group.</li>\n</
 ul>\n</div>\n<div><hr class="styled-hr"></div>\n<div class="paragraph"><st
 rong><span style="font-size: medium\;">Data Collection Ideas</span></stron
 g>\n<ul>\n<li>Values and Attitude Surveys and Questionnaires</li>\n<li>Int
 erviews</li>\n<li>Fieldwork</li>\n<li>Ecosystem Modelling</li>\n<li>Models
  (physical\, software\, mathematical)</li>\n<li>Field Manipulation Experim
 ents</li>\n<li>Lab Work</li>\n<li>EIAs</li>\n<li>Secondary Data (must use 
 unique data)</li>\n<li>Qualitative and Quantitate Data</li>\n<li>A Combina
 tion of any of the above</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div><hr class="styled-hr"><
 /div>\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><span style="font-size: medium\;">Un
 derstanding Quantitative and Qualitative Data</span></strong>\n<ol>\n<li><
 strong>Quantitative Data</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>This type of data is numerica
 l and gathered through measurements. It can be analyzed using statistics a
 nd presented in tables\, graphs\, or maps.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong
 >Qualitative Data</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Collected through observations and j
 udgments\, qualitative data is non-numerical. It might be processed throug
 h coding\, quantified\, or presented in images or text. Keep the word limi
 t in mind if presenting as text.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Combining
  Data Types</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Many students convert qualitative data int
 o quantitative form to strengthen their analysis. For instance\, qualitati
 ve observations\, like the weather during data collection\, can help provi
 de context for the quantitative data.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Data
  Collection Methods for Qualitative Data</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>There are sev
 eral ways to gather qualitative data:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observation</stro
 ng>: Can be direct or participant-based.</li>\n<li><strong>Interviews</str
 ong>: Structured\, semi-structured\, or open-ended.</li>\n<li><strong>Ques
 tionnaires/Surveys</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Visual Media</strong>: Photo
 graphy\, film\, and other multimedia resources.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\
 nEnsuring that your data provides enough detail is crucial for meaningful 
 analysis and clear conclusions connected to your research question.</li>\n
 </ol>\n<p><img src="https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/376418/files/4494
 8326/preview" alt="image.png" loading="lazy" data-api-endpoint="https://uc
 ps.instructure.com/api/v1/courses/376418/files/44948326" data-api-returnty
 pe="File"></p>\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><span style="font-size: med
 ium\;">Guidelines for Using Surveys and Questionnaires<br>​</span></stro
 ng><br>​Survey research involves the collection of information from a sa
 mple of individuals through their responses to questions.. It is an&nbsp\;
 efficient method for systematically collecting data from a broad spectrum 
 of individuals and educational settings<br>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Data Volume
 </strong>: Aim for enough responses to achieve statistical validity. A 20
 –30% response rate is typical\, so plan accordingly to meet minimum data
  needs.</li>\n<li><strong>Question Design</strong>: Structure questions to
  align with your data analysis method. Start with general demographic ques
 tions\, followed by questions related to the independent variable and pote
 ntial confounding variables.</li>\n<li><strong>Sample Size</strong>: For s
 trong statistical outcomes\, collect at least 30 responses per category of
  the independent variable (e.g.\, age cohorts).</li>\n<li><strong>Statisti
 cal Processing</strong>:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Descriptive</strong>: Use freq
 uency\, percentages\, and measures of central tendency and dispersion.</li
 >\n<li><strong>Inferential</strong>: Consider t-tests\, chi-squared tests\
 , ANOVA\, correlation\, and regression for deeper analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<
 /li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class="paragraph">Click<span>&nbsp\;</span><u><a
  href="https://www.mrgscience.com/surveys.html">here</a></u><span>&nbsp\;<
 /span>for more information on creating surveys and questionnaires<br><br><
 /div>\n<div><hr class="styled-hr"></div>\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><
 span style="font-size: medium\;">Guidelines for Using Databases and Simula
 tions<br>​</span></strong><br>​​It is possible to use databases as t
 he source for IA investigations\, though this would need to be carefully m
 anaged.&nbsp\;A challenge with using informatics/databases in IA work will
  be generating quality questions that can be explored effectively. However
 \, this must be a unique analysis.<br>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Methodology and 
 Purpose</strong>: Explain why you chose a specific database or simulation 
 and how it aligns with your research question. Clearly outline the method 
 used for data collection.</li>\n<li><strong>Source Identification</strong>
 : Identify data sources\, evaluate their reliability\, and confirm their r
 elevance to your research question.</li>\n<li><strong>Documentation</stron
 g>: Include screenshots of the database or simulation with URLs or program
  names to show data collection and manipulation steps.</li>\n<li><strong>M
 ultiple Sources</strong>: Use more than one database if possible for compr
 ehensive data. If limited to one\, explain why.</li>\n<li><strong>Data Fil
 tering and Selection</strong>: Describe how you controlled\, extracted\, o
 r filtered data and justify your selection criteria. Screenshots can help 
 illustrate these steps.</li>\n<li><strong>Simulation Consistency</strong>:
  Avoid simulations that produce identical values each time. Use additional
  sources or simulations to gather varied data.</li>\n<li><strong>Computati
 onal Analysis</strong>: Use tools that provide accurate\, quantifiable dat
 a. For example\, convert visual elements (like color saturation) into nume
 rical values where possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Field Data Databases</stron
 g>: Databases with field data are acceptable\; focus your method on how yo
 u used the database rather than on the original data collection process.</
 li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div class="paragraph">Click<u><span>&nbsp\;</span><a 
 href="https://www.mrgscience.com/secondary-data---data-bases.html">here</a
 ><span>&nbsp\;</span></u>for more information on using Databases and sever
 al database resources<br><br></div>\n<div><hr class="styled-hr"></div>\n<d
 iv class="paragraph"><strong><span style="font-size: medium\;">Data Visual
 ization Techniques for Qualitative Data:</span></strong>\n<ul>\n<li><stron
 g>Charts</strong>: Pie\, bar\, histogram\, Gantt\, area\, scatter\, bullet
 </li>\n<li><strong>Maps</strong>: Heat map\, choropleth map</li>\n<li><str
 ong>Graphs</strong>: Box and whisker plot\, waterfall chart</li>\n<li><str
 ong>Diagrams</strong>: Network diagram\, correlation matrix</li>\n<li><str
 ong>Other</strong>: Pictogram\, timeline\, highlight table\, word cloud</l
 i>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<div><hr class="styled-hr"></div>\n<div class="paragrap
 h"><strong><span style="font-size: medium\;">Putting It All Together</span
 ><br><br>​Hypothesis:</strong><br>Although not required by the IB Organi
 zation\, for many investigations it is appropriate for you to include a hy
 pothesis.&nbsp\; A hypothesis is like a prediction.&nbsp\; It will often t
 ake the form of a proposed relationship between two or more variables that
  can be tested by experiment:&nbsp\; “If X is done\, then Y will occur.
 ”&nbsp\;<br><br>No all investigations will have a hypothesis. However\, 
 they help you focus your ideas. Be sure that your hypothesis is related di
 rectly to your research question<br><br>Also justify your hypothesis. This
  should be a brief discussion (paragraph form) about the theory or ‘why
 ’ behind the hypothesis and prediction.&nbsp\; Be sure the hypothesis is
  related directly to the research question and that the manipulated and re
 sponding variables for the experiment are clear.&nbsp\;<br><br><strong>Met
 hodology:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>IV correctly identified with units and level
 s\, including how the levels were chosen.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>Minimum 
 of five levels of IV over a suitable range (unless comparing populations o
 r correlating variables without manipulation).</li>\n<li>DV (as directly r
 ecorded and/or calculated) correctly identified with units.</li>\n<li>Impo
 rtant CV identified\, with the potential impact of each discussed.&nbsp\; 
 Validity measures and/or control group are not misunderstood as CV.</li>\n
 <li>List or photo of apparatus and materials including size\, graduation a
 nd uncertainty.</li>\n<li>Reference to preliminary trials\, if completed.<
 /li>\n<li>Method to change and measure IV fully detailed (including tools\
 , units and uncertainty).</li>\n<li>Method for measuring DV fully detailed
  (including tools\, units and uncertainty).​</li>\n<li>Sufficient repeat
 s of DV measurement to ensure reliability and allow for statistics (5 for 
 SD\, ​10 for T-test\, 20+for correlation).</li>\n<li>Collection of data 
 from other students or sources is explained and referenced.</li>\n<li>If s
 ampling only a portion of a population\, include the method for ensuring t
 he sample was randomly selected.</li>\n<li>Method for maintaining and meas
 uring CV is detailed (including tools\, units and uncertainty).​</li>\n<
 li>Method includes validity measures to ensure experimental measurements a
 re valid and consistent.​</li>\n<li>Method is clear\, specific and easil
 y replicated as described.​</li>\n<li>Full citation of a published proto
 col (or elements of)\, if used.</li>\n</ul>\n<br>Make a list of materials 
 needed.&nbsp\;\n<ul>\n<li>Be as specific as possible (example:&nbsp\; “5
 0 mL beaker instead of ‘beaker’).&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>A well labeled diag
 ram or photograph of how the experiment is set up may be appropriate.</li>
 \n<li>Be sure the diagram includes a title and any necessary labels.</li>\
 n</ul>\n<br>State or discuss the method (procedure) that was used in the e
 xperiment.&nbsp\;\n<ul>\n<li>should be in the form of a step-by-step direc
 tion.&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>provide enough detail so that another person could 
 repeat your work by reading the report!&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>you don’t have 
 to go into detail about standard\, well-understood actions. If a standard 
 technique is used\, it should be referenced.</li>\n<li>if something is don
 e in the procedure to minimize an anticipated error\, mention this as well
 .&nbsp\; (Example:&nbsp\; “Carefully cutting plant stem under water to r
 educe affect of air on transpiration rate.”)&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>clearly st
 ate how to collect data.&nbsp\; What measuring device was used\, what data
  was recorded and when? Or what qualitative observations were looked for (
 such as color change)?</li>\n<li>must allow collection of sufficient relev
 ant data.&nbsp\; As a rule\, the lower limit is five measurements\, or a s
 ample size of five. Very small samples run from 5 to 20\, small samples ru
 n from 20 to 30\, and big samples run from 30 upwards. Obviously\, this wi
 ll vary within the limits of the time available for an investigation.</li>
 \n</ul>\n<br>“Control of variables” refers to the manipulation of the 
 independent variable and the attempt to maintain the controlled variables 
 at a constant value.\n<ul>\n<li>describe how the control of variables is a
 chieved. If the control of variables is not practically possible\, some ef
 fort should be made to monitor the variable(s).&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>st
 ate an explicit procedure or method for how each variable will be controll
 ed and monitored. ​</li>\n<li>if using a known experimental protocol\, y
 ou must explain how you modified the standard method to make it your own.&
 nbsp\;</li>\n</ul>\n​<br><strong>Justification of Sampling:</strong>\n<u
 l>\n<li>Justify your method</li>\n<li>Indicate why you choose to collect d
 ata the way you did</li>\n<li>Verify that the data is random and unbiased 
 (specifically in survey data)</li>\n<li>Indicate how you made sure to coll
 ect sufficient and relevant data</li>\n</ul>\n<br><strong>Safety\, Ethics 
 and Environmental Issues:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>​Safety issues fully consi
 dered (including human consent forms if needed).</li>\n<li>Ethical issues 
 fully considered (including animal experimentation policy if&nbsp\; needed
 ).</li>\n<li>Environmental issues fully considered (such as reduction of w
 aste and disposal of chemicals).</li>\n<li>List any safety precautions tha
 t must be taken during the lab\, including personal and environmental conc
 erns.&nbsp\;</li>\n<li>Many ESS studies will involve ethical or safety con
 siderations. You&nbsp\;must address this\, where necessary\, paying attent
 ion to the IB animal experimentation policy (which includes guidelines on 
 working with human subjects)\, and should write about their strategies for
  upholding safety and/or ethical standards in the report.</li>\n</ul>\n<di
 v class="paragraph"><strong>Risk Assessment Forms</strong></div>\n<div>\n<
 div class="wsite-multicol">\n<div class="wsite-multicol-table-wrap">\n<tab
 le class="wsite-multicol-table">\n<tbody class="wsite-multicol-tbody">\n<t
 r class="wsite-multicol-tr">\n<td class="wsite-multicol-col">\n<div>\n<div
  class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none "><a><img src="https://www.mrg
 science.com/uploads/2/0/7/9/20796234/screen-shot-2020-04-29-at-12-33-34-pm
 _orig.png" alt="Picture" width="407" height="195" loading="lazy"></a></div
 >\n</div>\n</td>\n<td class="wsite-multicol-col">\n<div>\n<div class="wsit
 e-image wsite-image-border-none "><a><img src="https://www.mrgscience.com/
 uploads/2/0/7/9/20796234/published/bduk-33-raid-log-15-risk-matrix.png?158
 8177851" alt="Picture" loading="lazy"></a></div>\n</div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</
 tbody>\n</table>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class="paragraph">In line w
 ith the poster&nbsp\;<strong><em>Ethical practice in the Diploma Programme
 </em>\,</strong>&nbsp\;the following guidelines exist for all practical wo
 rk undertaken as part of the Diploma Programme.\n<ul>\n<li>No experiments 
 involving other people will be undertaken without their written consent an
 d their&nbsp\;understanding of the nature of the experiment.</li>\n<li>No 
 experiment will be undertaken that inflicts pain on\, or causes distress t
 o humans or live animals.</li>\n<li>No experiment or fieldwork will be und
 ertaken that damages the environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<br>Find out more in&nb
 sp\;<u><a href="https://www.mrgscience.com/uploads/2/0/7/9/20796234/ib_ani
 mal_policy.pdf" target="_blank">Animal Experimentation</a></u></div>\n<div
  class="paragraph">\n<div class="paragraph"><strong><span style="font-size
 : medium\;">Measurement Precision</span></strong></div>\n<div class="parag
 raph">Unless there is a digital display\, always measure to&nbsp\;one digi
 t beyond&nbsp\;the&nbsp\;smallest unit of CERTAIN measurement of the tool.
 &nbsp\;&nbsp\;For&nbsp\;example\, if you use a ruler that can accurately m
 easure to the&nbsp\;tenth&nbsp\;of&nbsp\;a centimeter\, your measurement w
 ould be to the hundredth of a&nbsp\;centimeter. &nbsp\;The number of signi
 ficant&nbsp\;digits&nbsp\;should reflect the precision of the&nbsp\;measur
 ement.<br><br>There&nbsp\;should&nbsp\;be no&nbsp\;variation in the precis
 ion of raw data. The&nbsp\;same&nbsp\;number of digits past the decimal pl
 ace&nbsp\;should be&nbsp\;used. &nbsp\;For data&nbsp\;derived from&nbsp\;p
 rocessing raw data&nbsp\;(i.e.\, means)\,&nbsp\;the level of&nbsp\;precisi
 on&nbsp\;should be&nbsp\;consistent with that of the&nbsp\;raw&nbsp\;data.
 </div>\n<div>\n<div class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none "><a><img s
 rc="https://www.mrgscience.com/uploads/2/0/7/9/20796234/cnx-chem-01-05-mea
 sure_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" width="707" height="404" loading="lazy"></a><
 /div>\n</div>\n<div class="paragraph">​You may need to estimate the degr
 ee of precision sometimes especially with stop watches. Digital stop watch
 es are said to be&nbsp\;accurate to 0.01s but human reaction time is only 
 +/-0.1s.<br><br>For electronic probes you may have to go to the manufactur
 er's specifications (on their web site or in the instructions manual).<br>
 ​<br><em><strong>​</strong></em><em><strong>Uncertainty</strong></em><
 br>All&nbsp\;measurements&nbsp\;have&nbsp\;uncertainties&nbsp\;and are onl
 y as accurate as the tool being used. For&nbsp\;general purposes\, the&nbs
 p\;accuracy of a&nbsp\;measurement&nbsp\;device is&nbsp\;one half of&nbsp\
 ;the smallest&nbsp\; measurement&nbsp\;possible with the&nbsp\;device.&nbs
 p\;&nbsp\;To determine uncertainty:\n<ul>\n<li>Find the smallest increment
  of measurement on your measurement device</li>\n<li>Divide it by two</li>
 \n<li>Round to the first non-zero number</li>\n</ul>\n<br>So\, for&nbsp\;e
 xample\, the&nbsp\;rulers in class&nbsp\;measure to the&nbsp\; millimeter 
 (0.10 cm).&nbsp\;&nbsp\;Therefore\,&nbsp\;the&nbsp\;ruler’s measurement&
 nbsp\;uncertainty is&nbsp\;+/- 0.05&nbsp\;cm. &nbsp\;​<br><br>The numeri
 cal value of a ± uncertainty value tells you the range of the result. For
  example a result reported as 1.23 ± 0.05 means that the experimenter has
  some degree of confidence that the true value falls in between 1.18 and 1
 .28.<br>​<br>Examples:&nbsp\;\n<ul>\n<li>Mass of a penny&nbsp\;on&nbsp\;
 a&nbsp\;centigram balance:&nbsp\; 3.12g&nbsp\;(+/-&nbsp\;0.05g)&nbsp\;</li
 >\n<li>Temperature using a&nbsp\;typical lab&nbsp\;thermometer:&nbsp\; 25.
 5°C (+/-&nbsp\; 0.5°C)&nbsp\;</li>\n</ul>\n​<br>Experimental&nbsp\;<st
 rong>uncertainties should be rounded UP to one significant figure</strong>
 . Uncertainties are almost always quoted to one&nbsp\;significant digit an
 d we round up because it’s better to suggest higher uncertainty than to 
 imply there is less uncertainty.\n<ul>\n<li>Wrong: ± 12.5 mL</li>\n<li>Co
 rrect: ± 20 mL</li>\n</ul>\n<br>The measurement should have the same numb
 er of digits (decimal places) as the uncertainty. It&nbsp\; would be confu
 sing to suggest that you knew the digit in the hundredths (or&nbsp\;thousa
 ndths) place when you admit that you unsure of the tenths place.&nbsp\;\n<
 ul>\n<li>Wrong: 1.2<strong>37</strong>&nbsp\;s ± 0.1 s</li>\n<li>Correct:
  1.2 s ± 0.1 s&nbsp\;</li>\n</ul>\n<br>​Just as for&nbsp\;units\, in a&
 nbsp\;column of data&nbsp\;students&nbsp\;can show the&nbsp\;uncertainty i
 n the column heading&nbsp\;and&nbsp\;don’t&nbsp\;have to&nbsp\;keep re-w
 riting if for every measurement in the&nbsp\;table.&nbsp\;<br><br><strong>
 Units</strong><br>The&nbsp\;system of units used in science is called the 
 International System of Units (SI units). &nbsp\;In the table&nbsp\;below 
 are&nbsp\;some of the more&nbsp\;common SI&nbsp\;units</div>\n<div>\n<div 
 class="wsite-image wsite-image-border-none "><a><img src="https://www.mrgs
 cience.com/uploads/2/0/7/9/20796234/ap6899_orig.jpg" alt="Picture" width="
 695" height="463" loading="lazy"></a></div>\n</div>\n<div class="paragraph
 ">The&nbsp\;following example shows different ways to&nbsp\;express the sa
 me&nbsp\;unit.&nbsp\;\n<ul>\n<li>Oxygen consumption&nbsp\;(millilters per 
 gram per&nbsp\;hour)</li>\n<li>Oxygen&nbsp\; consumption&nbsp\;(ml/g/h)</l
 i>\n<li>Oxygen&nbsp\; consumption&nbsp\;(ml g-1 h-1)</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n
 </div>\n</div>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n</div>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>
END:VEVENT
