DESCRIPTION:Why was Brazil affected by the Great Depression?\n-------------
 -----------------------------------\n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructu
 re.com/courses/368381/files/41326669/preview)\n\nCoffee being embarked in 
 the Port of Santos\,  São Paulo\, 1880\n\nHistorical Context:\n\nThe res
 ults of the depression on Brazil by 1931 was severe:\n\n* As most of the p
 opulation was dependent on agriculture\, the economic slump forced many ou
 t of work and ruined many of the large planters some of whom had taken out
  large loans before the crash in the expectation of guaranteed profits\n\n
 * Unemployment in the cities grew\n\n* Fall in income in all sections of s
 ociety meant a fall in demand for goods which further affected industry\n\
 n* International trade was further affected when the Brazilian government 
 suspended payments on debt in September 1931 and imposed restrictions to p
 revent investors selling off currency\n\n* Between 1929 and 1931\, Brazil
 ’s GDP fell by 4%. In the ensuing two years it fell another 5%.\n\nAccor
 ding to this source from a modern text book\, consider why Brazil was affe
 cted by Depression following the Wall Street Crash.\n\n"Before 1929\, Braz
 il was mainly an agricultural country. By the 1920s the main crop was coff
 ee and Brazil had a strong hold over the world market as it provided 70% o
 f all coffee consumed. Indeed 70% of Brazil’s exports during the 1920s c
 ame from coffee.\n\nThis very dependence on one commodity meant that it wa
 s vulnerable\; although some diversification into industry was beginning\,
  the federal government had provided little direction or support in this a
 rea. Nor had the government tried to regulate production of the economy or
  strengthen the infrastructure. This was also due to the opposition of the
  plantation-owning oligarchy.\n\nBy 1929\, Brazil had a foreign debt of ar
 ound 900 million dollars. Thus the Brazilian economy was highly vulnerable
  to the events of 1929 when coffee prices crashed from 22.5 cents a pound 
 in 1929 to 8 cents in 1931.\n\nA combination of good harvests and fall in 
 demand for coffee left  Brazil was left with large reserves of unsold cof
 fee as well as huge debts. Brazil's industry (which was not in any case th
 at strong) was also affected as demand fell not just from within Brazil bu
 t also from other Latin American countries which had been affected by the 
 Depression. With a shrinking domestic and export market Brazil's industria
 l areas\, particularly in the south of the country\, went into decline."\n
 \nAssignment Directions: \n\n* With your group\, investigate the politica
 l\, economic and social structures in Brazil from 1890 to 1920.\n\n* Recor
 d your findings [in the following document] (https://docs.google.com/docum
 ent/d/1aKwMqpv_X1u_zSlrovGPC8tNiVQJX4aFK638rGWBooQ/copy).\n\n* Your textbo
 ok (starting on pg. 208) will be a good source\, as will [Britannica] (htt
 ps://www.britannica.com/place/Brazil/The-coffee-presidents#ref25047). You 
 may also use any other sources you wish.\n\n* Save your work with your not
 es for future reference.\n\n* Upload your document to Canvas when complete
 . 
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Brazil and the Depression [IB History of the Americas HL - Tesar - 
 YL - 25/26]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://ucps.instructure.com/calendar?include_contexts=course
 _368381&month=03&year=2026#assignment_6479744
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h3 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Why w
 as Brazil affected by the Great Depression?</strong></h3>\n<p><span><img s
 rc="https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/368381/files/41326669/preview" al
 t="image.png" data-api-endpoint="https://ucps.instructure.com/api/v1/cours
 es/368381/files/41326669" data-api-returntype="File" loading="lazy"></span
 ></p>\n<p><span style="font-size: 10pt\;">Coffee being embarked in the Por
 t of Santos\,&nbsp\; São Paulo\, 1880</span></p>\n<p><strong>Historical C
 ontext:</strong></p>\n<p><span>The results of the depression on Brazil by 
 1931 was severe:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>As most of the population was
  dependent on agriculture\, the economic slump forced many out of work and
  ruined many of the large planters some of whom had taken out large loans 
 before the crash in the expectation of guaranteed profits</span></li>\n<li
 ><span>Unemployment in the cities grew</span></li>\n<li><span>Fall in inco
 me in all sections of society meant a fall in demand for goods which furth
 er affected industry</span></li>\n<li><span>International trade was furthe
 r affected when the Brazilian government suspended payments on debt in Sep
 tember 1931 and imposed restrictions to prevent investors selling off curr
 ency</span></li>\n<li><span>Between 1929 and 1931\, Brazil’s GDP fell by
  4%. In the ensuing two years it fell another 5%.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><
 span>According to this source from a modern text book\, consider why Brazi
 l was affected by Depression following the Wall Street Crash.</span></p>\n
 <p><span><em>"Before 1929\, Brazil was mainly an agricultural country. By 
 the 1920s the main crop was coffee and Brazil had a strong hold over the w
 orld market as it provided 70% of all coffee consumed. Indeed 70% of Brazi
 l’s exports during the 1920s came from coffee.</em></span></p>\n<p><span
 ><em>This very dependence on one commodity meant that it was vulnerable\; 
 although some diversification into industry was beginning\, the federal go
 vernment had provided little direction or support in this area. Nor had th
 e government tried to regulate production of the economy or strengthen the
  infrastructure. This was also due to the opposition of the plantation-own
 ing oligarchy.</em></span></p>\n<p><span><em>By 1929\, Brazil had a foreig
 n debt of around 900 million dollars. Thus the Brazilian economy was highl
 y vulnerable to the events of 1929 when coffee prices crashed from 22.5 ce
 nts a pound in 1929 to 8 cents in 1931.</em></span></p>\n<p><span><em>A co
 mbination of good harvests and fall in demand for coffee left&nbsp\; Brazi
 l was left with large reserves of unsold coffee as well as huge debts. Bra
 zil's industry (which was not in any case that strong) was also affected a
 s demand fell not just from within Brazil but also from other Latin Americ
 an countries which had been affected by the Depression. With a shrinking d
 omestic and export market Brazil's industrial areas\, particularly in the 
 south of the country\, went into decline."</em></span></p>\n<p><strong>Ass
 ignment Directions:</strong>&nbsp\;</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>With your group\,
  investigate the political\, economic and social structures in Brazil from
  1890 to 1920.</span></li>\n<li><span>Record your findings <a href="https:
 //docs.google.com/document/d/1aKwMqpv_X1u_zSlrovGPC8tNiVQJX4aFK638rGWBooQ/
 copy">in the following document</a>.</span></li>\n<li><span>Your textbook 
 (starting on pg. 208) will be a good source\, as will <a href="https://www
 .britannica.com/place/Brazil/The-coffee-presidents#ref25047">Britannica</a
 >. You may also use any other sources you wish.</span></li>\n<li><span>Sav
 e your work with your notes for future reference.</span></li>\n<li><span>U
 pload your document to Canvas when complete.&nbsp\;</span></li>\n</ul>
END:VEVENT
