 he heart of the sea</span></a></p>\n<p id="E240" class="qowt-li-0_0 qowt-l
 ist qowt-stl-ListParagraph x-scope qowt-word-para-1" style="padding-left: 
 40px\;" role="listitem"><span id="E241">After reading all the articles abo
 ut what is your perspective of whaling? Think about this from an </span><s
 pan id="E243">ecocentric</span><span id="E245">\, </span><span id="E247">a
 nthropcentric</span><span id="E249"> or technocentric viewpoint.</span><sp
 an id="E250"> You must justify your answer.</span></p>\n<p id="E268" class
 ="x-scope qowt-word-para-1"><span id="E269">3) Read the following paragrap
 h from a BBC </span><a id="E271" href="http://www.bbc.co.uk/earth/story/20
 151203-why-do-some-countries-still-hunt-whales" target="_blank"><span id="
 E272" class="qowt-stl-Hyperlink">“Why Do Some Countries Still Hunt Whale
 s”</span></a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div id="pageBorders" class="style-sc
 ope qowt-page"></div>\n<div id="contentsContainer" class="style-scope qowt
 -page">\n<div id="contents" class="style-scope qowt-page">\n<p id="E275" c
 lass="x-scope qowt-word-para-1" style="padding-left: 40px\;"><span id="E27
 7">Minke</span><span id="E279"> whales are found in all of the world's oce
 ans. The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) lists them 
 as "least concern"\, meaning they are not in any imminent danger of extinc
 tion. The IUCN estimates that there are over 100\,000 </span><span id="E28
 1">minke</span><span id="E283"> whales in the wild\, so it should be possi
 ble to continue hunting them without endangering the species.</span></p>\n
 <p class="x-scope qowt-word-para-1" style="padding-left: 40px\;"><span id=
 "E286">Does this change your opinion</span><span id="E287"> from the previ
