BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20260226T180800Z
UID:event-assignment-7096149
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260513
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Foundation & Framing\n--------------------\n\nObjective: Stude
 nts will understand the purpose and expectations of the EE Cafe and begin 
 refining their RQs by aligning them with a subject-specific methodology. 
 \n\nINTRODUCTION TO THE EE CAFE\n---------------------------\n\nWhat is th
 e EE Cafe? \n---------------------\n\nA showcase event for your EE-in-pro
 gress\n\nDesigned for feedback\, reflection\, and public sharing\n\nAudien
 ce: teachers\, students\, parents\, and administrators\n\nExpectations:\n\
 nVisual representation of your research (poster\, diagram\, presentations\
 , display board\, video)\n\nVisual explanation (short\, confident summary 
 of your research topic) and the following elements that you can answer que
 stions about:\n\n* RQ and Methodology\n\n* Context and Literature Review\n
 \n* Research findings so far\n\n* Chapter headings or structure (should co
 ntain elements from RQ)\n\n* Problems encountered\n\n* Bibliography (with 
 accurate formatting)\n\n[UNDERSTANDING METHODOLOGIES or APPROACHES BY SUBJ
 ECT Links to an external site.] (https://resources.finalsite.net/images/v1
 616130909/hzscishisnet/mxrti5oecbh4f7afd34y/IB-Extended-Essay-Subject-Spec
 ific-Guide.pdf)\n---------------------------------------------------------
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 --------------------------------------------------------------------------
 --------\n\nLiterature & Language\n\nThe essay should involve analysis of 
 the text(s) in the light of the RQ. Students may also include a critical p
 erspective on secondary source material so that the views of critics are u
 sed to support the students' own arguments.\n\nSciences: Biology\n\nStuden
 ts must demonstrate that their chosen methods and materials are appropriat
 e for addressing the RQ. They should explain their rationale for selecting
  practical methods. If they undertake experimental work\, they must includ
 e sufficient information on their methodology for their work to be repeate
 d.\n\nIndividuals & Societies: History\n\nWhere possible\, an attempt shou
 ld be made to use both primary and secondary sources. They should demonstr
 ate both factual material and the opinions of historians. These sources mu
 st provide sufficient material to develop and support an argument and conc
 lusion relevant to the research question. \n\nThe Arts: Visual Arts\n\nIf
  students choose a popular topic well researched by others\, they must att
 empt to structure an RQ that may lead to a new point of view\, focus\, or 
 interpretation. Sources are expected to include visual images\, some of wh
 ich (depending on research) may be the students' photographs.\n\nWhat kind
 s of data or evidence are valid in your subject?\n\nHow do researchers or 
 experts in your subject frame or answer questions?\n\nWhat limitations mig
 ht apply to search in your subject?\n\nALIGNING RQ WITH METHODS\n---------
 ---------------\n\nTake a look at your research question:\n\nWhat is your 
 subject's methodology\, and does your RQ lend itself to it? \n\nExamples:
 \n\nLiterature & Language\n\nRQ: How does the use of unreliable narrators 
 in Gothic fiction reflect cultural anxieties of the 19th century?\n\n→ M
 ethod: Close textual analysis + application of historical literary theory\
 n\nBiology\n\nRQ: What effect does caffeine concentration have on the hea
 rt rate of Daphnia magna?\n\n→ Method: Controlled lab experiment with me
 asurable variables\n\nVisual Arts\n\nRQ: To what extent does Yayo Kusama'
 s art express trauma through repetition? \n\n→ Method: Visual analysis 
 + psychological/artistic theory application\n\nWork Time \n----------\n\n
 Task 1: Literature Review Questions\n-----------------------------------\n
 \n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/294174/files/40052632/
 preview)\n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/294174/files
 /40052651/preview)\n\nAnswer these questions to define the conversation su
 rrounding your topic. This will help you prepare for both the background a
 nd methodology of your EE. \n\nComplete the Literature Review Questions\n
 \n[Literature Assignment.pdf] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/294174
 /files/40051713?wrap=1)[ Download Literature Assignment.pdf] (https://ucps
 .instructure.com/courses/294174/files/40051713/download?download_frd=1)\n\
 nTask 2: Visual Planning\n-----------------------\n\nSketch an early versi
 on of their visual representation (poster\, diagram\, presentation\, displ
 ay board\, video)\n\nConsider the following:\n\nWhat will your audience le
 arn when they stop at your station?\n\nWhat visuals\, headings\, or quotes
  could you include?\n\nHow to break down an RQ for the argument and possib
 le chapter heading for the essay structure:\n\nVisual Arts\n\nRQ: How doe
 s Yayoi Kusama use repetition in her infinity Mirror Rooms to express psyc
 hological trauma? \n\nArgument from RQ: Kusama's repeated use of pattern
  and space reflects inner psychological states\, especially trauma and obs
 ession.\n\nSubject-Specific Notes (Criterion A)\n\nMust include analysis o
 f visual elements and techniques\n\nMost connect artistic choices to cult
 ural and/or personal context\n\nPossible Chapter Headings:\n\n1. Introduct
 ion and Method\n\n2. Kusama's Biography and Mental Health\n\n3. Repetition
  as Visual Language\n\n4. Case Study: Infinity Mirror Rooms\n\n5. Trauma a
 nd Perception in Spatial Art\n\n6. Conclusion\n\nComputer Science\n\nRQ: 
 To what extent does implementing a neural network improve the accuracy of 
 handwriting recognition compared to traditional algorithms?\n\nArgument fr
 om RQ: Neural networks offer a more effective approach for recognizing ha
 ndwritten characters than older\, rule-based models. \n\nSubject-Subject 
 Specific Notes (Criterion A)\n\nMust involve original coding or system de
 sign\n\nIncludes clear definition of success metrics (e.g.\, accuracy\, 
 speed)\n\nPossible Chapter Headings:\n\n1. Introduction and Justification\
 n\n2. Overview of Handwriting Recognition Techniques\n\n3. Design and Impl
 ementation of Neural Network\n\n4. Benchmarking Against Traditional Algori
 thms\n\n5. Evaluation of Results and Accuracy\n\n6. Conclusion and Implica
 tions\n\nLiterature and Language\n\nRQ: How does George Orwell use langua
 ge to manipulate public opinion in Animal Farm?\n\nArgument in RQ: Orwel
 l uses specific rhetorical and linguistic techniques to show how language 
 can be used to manipulate the masses. \n\nSubject-Specific Notes (Criteri
 on A)\n\nMust focus on close reading and stylistic analysis\n\nPossible Ch
 apter Headings:\n\n1. Introduction and Method (Language and Power Theory)\
 n\n2. Language and Propaganda in Orwell's Context\n\n3. Rhetorical Techniq
 ues Used by Squealer\n\n4. Repetition and Simplification as Tools of Contr
 ol\n\n5. Symbolism and Language of Authority\n\n6. Conclusion\n\nHistory\n
 \nRQ: To what extent did the British government's portrayal of the Blitz 
 during WWII differ from civilian experiences? \n\nArgument in RQ: The Br
 itish government promoted a unified image of resilience that did not refle
 ct the actual diversity and difficulty of civilian life.\n\nSubject-Specif
 ic Notes (Criterion A)\n\nMust use primary sources\n\nShould frame a focu
 sed historical period/location\n\nPossible Chapter Headings\n\n1. Introduc
 tion and Source Scope\n\n2. Official Propaganda Objectives\n\n3. Depiction
  of the Blitz in Government Media\n\n4. Civilian Diaries and Oral Testimon
 ies\n\n5. Class and Regional Differences in Experience\n\n6. Conclusion: R
 eality vs. Representation\n\nWill your method be represented visually (gra
 ph\, diagram\, photos)?\n\nBegin drafting visual outline of presentation l
 ayout (poster/slides/diagram format)\n\nCheckpoint:\n-----------\n\nSubmit
  draft answers to Literature Review Questions\n\nSubmit RQ & methodology i
 n the same assignment\n\nFormative Grade: Criterion A (Focus and Method)\
 n\n* the research question\n\n* context that situates your question in exi
 sting knowledge (Literature Review)\; mention any theories\, approaches\, 
 factors that have already been suggested to answer your RQ\n\n* why your q
 uestion is worthy of investigation\; some reasons a question is worthy of 
 investigation could be that it has contemporary application\, offers a dif
 ferent interpretation\, issues explored are controversial\, uses a differe
 nt methodological approach\, clarifies existing misconceptions\, challenge
 s existing assumptions\, viewpoints\, or interpretations\, has not bene in
 vestigated before\, is important to the reading of a "text\," investigates
  the reliability or validity of a theory or approach\n\n* the source mater
 ial and/or scientific methodologies used\; outline your methodological or 
 source-based approach to show how you answered your RQ (be specific): \
 n\n* For scientific or other methodological approach:\n\n* Why did you cho
 ose this method?\n\n* How did this method help answer your question?\n\n* 
 For a source-based approach:\n\n* Why did you choose the sources you used?
 \n\n* How are they going to be used\, or how will you respond to their cla
 ims? \n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/294174/files/4
 0052814/preview)\n\n[image.png] (https://ucps.instructure.com/courses/2941
 74/files/40052682/preview)\n\n 
